\id ACT - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.4.1
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\rem ESFM v0.6 ACT
\rem WORDTABLE OET-LV_NT_word_table.tsv
\h Acts
\toc1 Acts
\toc2 Acts
\toc3 Acts
\mt2 The continued account by
\mt1 Lukas
\mt2 commonly called
\mt1 Acts
\is1 Introduction
\is2 Author
\ip This account commonly known as \bk The acts of the missionaries of Yeshua the Messiah\bk*, is the continuation of \bk The Good Message according to Lukas\bk*, and unlike most other Bibles, the \em Open English Translation\em* places these two accounts one after the other.
\is2 This account
\ip The main purpose of this account is to tell about the actions of the first followers of Yeshua (Jesus) the messiah. God’s spirit helped them to proclaim good message about him “in Yerushalem (Jerusalem) and all across \add the provinces of\add* Yudea (Judaea) and Shomron (Samaria), and all the way to the furthest parts of the planet” (1:8).
\ip This document has three divisions related to the expanding circles as the good message from Yeshua the messiah spread wider and wider: (1) In \bd Yerushalem\bd* where the believers start to get organised after Yeshua ascended up into the clouds; (2) the work and the message spreads wider out to all of the district of \bd Yehudah\bd*; and (3) spreading more widely again around the Mediterranean Ocean and all the way to \bd Romai\bd*.
\ip It’s also documented here about how God’s spirit came to the believers as they were together in a room in Yerushalem at the Festival of Pentecost (fifty days after The Passover). The spirit helps and gives wisdom and strength to the believers and teachers of believers.
\iot Main components of Lukas’s second account
\io1 The believers start to get organised \ior 1:1-26\ior*
\io2 a. The final command of Yeshua and his promise \ior 1:1-14\ior*
\io2 b. The replacing of Yehudah/Judas \ior 1:15-26\ior*
\io1 Witnessing in Yerushalem/Jerusalem \ior 2:1–8:3\ior*
\io1 Expanding out to Yehudah/Judaea and Shomron/Samaria \ior 8:4–12:25\ior*
\io1 Paulos’s activities \ior 13:1–28:31\ior*
\io2 a. Paulos’s first missionary trip \ior 13:1–14:28\ior*
\io2 b. The conference in Yerushalem \ior 15:1-35\ior*
\io2 c. Paulos’s second missionary trip \ior 15:36–18:22\ior*
\io2 d. Paulos’s third missionary trip \ior 18:23–21:16\ior*
\io2 e. Paulos is imprisoned in Yerushalem, taken to Kaisareia, and from there to Romai \ior 21:17–28:31\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\rem NOTE: This USFM file was exported from the ESFM source file with some loss of features, so don’t edit this derived file.
\c 1
\s1 Introduction
\p
\v 1 Dear Theofilos, I wrote \add my\add* first account\x + \xo 1:1: \xt Luk 1:1-4.\x* about all the things that \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* began to do and to teach
\v 2 until the day when he was taken up \add into the sky\add*\x + \xo 1:2: \xt Luk 24:51.\x* after, with the help of \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*, having commanded the missionaries that he had chosen.
\s1 Yeshua, alive, tells them to wait
\p
\v 3 After the time of his suffering, \add Yeshua\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* presented himself to them in ways that were infallible proofs \add that he was alive again\add*—appearing to them and telling them about \nd God’s\nd* kingdom.
\v 4 Once when they were together, he told them, “\wj Don’t leave Yerushalem \+add (Jerusalem)\+add*, but wait there for what was promised by the father, as you all have \+add already\+add* heard me say.\wj*\x + \xo 1:4: \xt Luk 24:49.\x*
\v 5 \wj Yohan \+add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\+add* immersed \+add people\+add* in water, but you’ll all be immersed in \+add the\+add* holy \+nd spirit\+nd* in just a few days.\wj*”\x + \xo 1:5: \xt Mat 3:11; Mrk 1:8; Luk 3:16; Yhn 1:33.\x*
\s1 Yeshua’s ascension
\p
\v 6 So when they were \add next\add* gathered together, they asked him, “\nd Master\nd*, is this now the time when you’ll be restoring the kingdom to Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*?”\f + \fr 1:6 \ft At the time of asking, Israelis were burdened under the rule and taxes of the Roman occupiers.\f*
\p
\v 7 But \add Yeshua\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* responded to them, “\wj You don’t need to know the times or seasons \+add when future events will happen\+add*—it’s the father who decides all that.\wj*
\v 8 \wj However, you all will be receiving power when the holy \+nd spirit\+nd* lands on you. Then you’ll tell \+add people\+add* about me in Yerushalem \+add (Jerusalem)\+add* and all across \+add the provinces of\+add* Yehudah \+add (Judea)\+add* and Shomron \+add (Samaria)\+add*, and all the way to the furthest \+add parts\+add* of the planet.\wj*”\x + \xo 1:8: \xt Mat 28:19; Mrk 16:15; Luk 24:47-48.\x*
\v 9 After he’d said that and while they were still looking at him, he was lifted up \add into the sky\add* until they could no longer see him because of the \add clouds\add*.\x + \xo 1:9: \xt Mrk 16:19; Luk 24:50-51.\x*
\p
\v 10 As they stood there staring into the sky after he’d gone, suddenly two men in white clothes were standing beside them
\v 11 and said, “You men from Galil! Why are you still standing there looking up at the sky? This \nd Yeshua\nd* who’s been taken up away from you into heaven will come back in the same way that you saw him go up into heaven.”
\s1 Matiyah replaces Yehudah Iskariot
\p
\v 12 Then they walked down the Mt. of Olives and returned to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* which was less than a kilometre away.
\v 13 They went into the upstairs room where they were staying—Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* and Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add*, Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add* and Andreas, Filippos \add (Philip)\add* and Toma \add (Thomas)\add*, Bar-Talmay \add (Bartholomew; Grk. Bartholomaios)\add* and Matay \add (Matthew; Grk. Matthaios)\add*, Ya’akov (son of Halfay \add (Grk. Halfaios)\add*), Shim’on \add (Simon)\add* (the Zealot member), and Yehudah \add (Judas; Grk. Yudas)\add* (son of Ya’akov).\x + \xo 1:13: \xt Mat 10:2-4; Mrk 3:16-19; Luk 6:14-16.\x*
\v 14 They were united in \add their\add* ongoing \add prayers\add*, along with Maria \add (Mary; Heb. Miryam; Grk. Maria/Mariam)\add* the mother \nd of Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, and his brothers.
\p
\v 15 Then one \add day\add*, Kefa stood up in the meeting of the believers (about 120 of them) and said,
\v 16 “Brothers, it was necessary for the scripture to be fulfilled, where the holy \nd spirit\nd* had spoken previously through David about Yehudah, the one who guided those that arrested \nd Yeshua\nd*
\v 17 because \add Yehudah\add* was one of us and took his part in \add our\add* work.
\p
\v 18 (\add Yehudah\add* had bought a field with the money he had received for his treachery, and when he had fallen down \add in that field\add*, his stomach had burst open and his intestines had slid out \add on the ground\add*.\x + \xo 1:18-19: \xt Mat 27:3-8.\x*
\v 19 So everyone in Yerushalem started calling that place ‘The field of blood’.)
\p
\v 20 Now it was written in the scroll of songs:
\q1 ‘Let his place become deserted with no one living there,’\x + \xo 1:20: \xt Psa 69:25.\x*
\m and
\q1 ‘Let another person take his position.’\x + \xo 1:20: \xt Psa 109:8.\x*
\p
\v 21 So now it’s appropriate for one of the men that has been with us for the whole time that the \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* was interacting with us,
\v 22 from Yeshua’s immersion by Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add*\x + \xo 1:22: \xt Mat 3:16; Mrk 1:9; Luk 3:21.\x* until the day that he was lifted up from us \add into the clouds\add*\x + \xo 1:22: \xt Mrk 16:19; Luk 24:51.\x*—a witness of the resurrection—to fill this position.
\p
\v 23 \add So\add* they selected two men: Yosef \add (Joseph)\add* Bar-Saba (nicknamed Yustos) and Matiyah \add (Grk. Matthias)\add*.
\v 24 Then they requested, “\nd Master\nd*, you know everyone’s inner thoughts, so reveal to us which of these two that you have chosen
\v 25 to take this position of service and being a missionary—the position that Yehudah \add (Judas; Grk. Yudas)\add* deserted to go to his own place.”
\v 26 \add So\add* they \add drew straws\add* and Matiyah was marked so he was chosen to join the eleven missionaries.
\c 2
\s1 God’s spirit arrives and many get saved
\p
\v 1 When the Day of Pentecost\x + \xo 2:1: \xt Lev 23:15-21; Deu 16:9-11.\x* \add actually\add* arrived, the believers met together in the same room.
\v 2 Suddenly there was a noise in the sky that sounded like a strong wind, then it filled the entire house where they were sitting.
\v 3 They saw what looked like tongues of fire that kept dividing\f + \fr 3:1 \ft See https://www.billmounce.com/monday-with-mounce/what-%E2%80%9Cdivided-tongue%E2%80%9D-acts-2-3-0\f* and then settling down on each one of them.
\v 4 In this way they were all filled \nd with the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* and began speaking in other languages as the \nd spirit\nd* enabled them.
\p
\v 5 At the time, there were godly Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* staying in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* that had come from other countries all around the world.
\v 6 When they heard this noise, lots of people came to investigate but they were confused because they heard the believers talking in their own \add languages\add* \add from around the world\add*.
\v 7 They were astounded and marvelled, asking, “Wow, aren’t these people talking all Galilim?
\v 8 \add So\add* how come we’re hearing them speak the \add languages\add* of the countries where we were born?
\v 9 There’s people here from Parthia, Maday \add (Media)\add*, Eylam, Mesopotamia, Yehudah \add (Judea)\add*, Kappadokia, Pontos, Asia \add Minor\add*,
\v 10 Frugia, Pamfulia, Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add*, Libya (near Kurainai \add (Cyrene)\add*), and visitors from Romai,
\v 11 Kraitai \add (Crete)\add*, and Arav. Some are Yehudim and some are non-Jews who’ve converted \add to Youdaismos\add*. And we can hear them talking \nd about God\nd* in our languages.”
\v 12 Everyone was amazed and totally puzzled, asking each other, “What’s going on here?”
\v 13 But others scoffed and said, “Ha, they’ve just had too much to drink.”
\s1 Kefa’s sermon
\p
\v 14 So the twelve stood up and Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* told the crowd in a loud voice, “People of Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* and everyone staying in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*. Listen to me so that I can explain this to you.
\v 15 This group isn’t drunk like you’re all thinking, because it’s \add still\add* mid-morning,
\v 16 but you’re seeing what the prophet Yoel \add (Joel)\add* wrote about:
\q1
\v 17 \nd God\nd* said, ‘In the last days I’ll pour out my \nd spirit\nd* on all humanity—your children will tell of future events, your young men will see visions, and your old men will have dreams \add from God\add*.
\v 18 I’ll pour my \nd spirit\nd* out on my male and female slaves and they’ll tell of future events.
\v 19 I’ll do unusual things with the stars and planets, and also down on the earth below: blood, fire, and clouds of smoke.
\v 20 I’ll make the sun go \add dark\add* and the moon go blood red. This will happen before the great victorious day \nd of the\nd* coming of \nd Yahweh\nd*.
\v 21 Then everyone who relies on the \add name and authority\add* \nd of Yahweh\nd* will be saved.’\x + \xo 2:17-21: \xt Yoel 2:28-32 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 22 “Fellow Israelis, listen to this account: You yourselves know that God did miracles through \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* from Nazaret, and he fulfilled signs \add from the scriptures\add*—in fact \nd God\nd* did this right in front of you.
\v 23 Then as part \nd of God’s\nd* plan and foreknowledge, this \add Yeshua\add* was taken to the authorities by those who didn’t follow their own laws, and you all killed him by having him \add nailed to\add* a post.\x + \xo 2:23: \xt Jhn 20:25.\x*\x + \xo 2:23: \xt Mat 27:35; Mrk 15:24; Luk 23:33; Yhn 19:18.\x*
\v 24 But \nd God\nd* released him from the agonies of death and \add brought him back to life\add*—death couldn’t restrain him.\x + \xo 2:24: \xt Mat 28:5-6; Mrk 16:6; Luk 24:5.\x*
\v 25 As David wrote,
\q1 ‘I foresaw \nd Yahweh\nd* always being \add in front of\add* me. I refuse to \add be afraid\add* because he’s there on my right.
\q1
\v 26 Because of that, I was happy and spoke out my praises. My whole body lives in anticipation
\v 27 because you’ll never abandon my soul in Haidais, nor will you allow the godly \add one\add* to decay.
\v 28 You showed me the roads to life. You fill me with gladness because of your presence.’\x + \xo 2:25-28: \xt Psa 16:8-11 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 29 “Fellow Israelis, we can tell you all with confidence that \add our\add* patriarch David both died and was buried, and his tomb is still here with us today.
\v 30 So because he was a prophet, and knowing that \nd God\nd* has promised him to seat one of his descendants on his throne,\x + \xo 2:30: \xt Psa 132:11; 2Sam 7:12-13.\x*
\v 31 he was foretelling the resurrection of the \nd messiah\nd* who was neither ‘abandoned in Haidais nor did his body decay.’
\v 32 Then \nd God\nd* raised this \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* back to life and we’re all witnesses of that.
\v 33 Now he’s sitting at \nd God’s\nd* right hand, and having received the promise of the holy \nd spirit\nd* from the father, he poured this out which you’re all seeing and hearing \add today\add*.
\v 34 You see, David never went up to the heavens, but he himself said,
\q1 ‘\nd Yahweh\nd* said to my \nd master\nd*: Sit down on my right
\q1
\v 35 until I defeat your enemies.’\x + \xo 2:34-35: \xt Psa 110:1.\x*
\p
\v 36 “So let all Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* know that this \nd Yeshua\nd* that you executed on a post, \nd God\nd* made him both \nd Yahweh\nd* and \nd messiah\nd*.”
\p
\v 37 When the people heard that, they were deeply troubled and asked Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* and the other missionaries, “What should we do then, brothers?”
\p
\v 38 Kefa responded, “Turn away \add from your sins\add* and each \nd one\nd* of you needs to be immersed by the \add authority\add* \nd of Yeshua the messiah\nd*. Then your sins \add will be forgiven\add* and you all will receive the gift of the holy \nd spirit\nd*,
\v 39 because that promise is to you all and your children and to their descendants—to anyone who will call out to our \nd God Yahweh\nd*.”
\p
\v 40 Then \add Kefa\add* shared further teaching with them and encouraged them to save themselves out from this corrupt generation.
\v 41 Because of that, those who welcomed his teaching were immersed and about three thousand people were added to their numbers that day.
\v 42 They continued listening to the teaching of the missionaries, and building their relationships with each other, and breaking bread and praying together.
\s1 The lifestyle of the early believers
\p
\v 43 They were serious in their learning about God, and the missionaries did many miracles and signs of God’s power \add in Yerushalem\add* \add (Jerusalem)\add* and all the people wanted to be right with God.
\v 44 All those who did believe were united in purpose and had shared use of many things.\x + \xo 2:44: \xt Acts 4:32-35.\x*
\v 45 Some sold their properties and possessions and divided them among the group so that everyone had everything they needed.
\v 46 Each day they \add worshipped\add* together at the temple and also went to various homes to break bread together. They talked about Yeshua \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* at their mealtimes without sophistication,
\v 47 praising \nd God\nd* and being popular with the people, and every day \nd Yahweh\nd* was adding more that were being saved.
\c 3
\s1 A lame man starts bouncing around
\p
\v 1 One afternoon around 3pm, Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* and Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* were going to the prayer meeting in the temple.
\v 2 Now there was a man who had been lame from birth, and every day he was placed beside a door into the temple—an entry named ‘Beautiful Door’. From there he could beg from the people entering the temple.
\v 3 He saw Kefa and Yohan about to enter into the temple, so he asked them for a donation,
\v 4 but Kefa and Yohan, looking closely at him said, “Look at us!”
\v 5 \add So\add* the man grabbed onto them, expecting to get given something by them.
\v 6 But Kefa said, “I don’t have any silver or gold, but I’ll give you what I do have. With the \add authority\add* \nd of Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd* from Nazaret, \add I command you\add* to walk.”
\v 7 Then \add Kefa\add* grabbed the man by the right hand and pulled him upwards. Immediately the man’s feet and ankles were strengthened
\v 8 and he jumped up and stood and started walking and went into the temple with them, walking and skipping and praising \nd God\nd*.
\v 9 All the people \add there\add* saw him walking and praising \nd God\nd*,
\v 10 and recognising that he was the man who usually sat \add outside\add* begging at the ‘Beautiful Door’ of the temple, they were astonished and astounded at what had happened to him.
\s1 Kefa’s sermon in the temple
\p
\v 11 As the man clung onto Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* and Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add*, all the people ran to see them in Shelomoh’s Porch and they were really astounded.
\v 12 But when Kefa saw \add the crowd\add*, he answered their thoughts, “Fellow Israelis, why are you all puzzled about this, and why are you staring at us as if this man is walking because of \em our\em* power or closeness to God.”
\v 13 The \nd god\nd* of Avraham, Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add*, and Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*—the \nd god\nd* of our fathers\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Exo 3:15.\x*—gave honour to his servant, \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, who you handed over \add to be killed\add* and then you rejected him when Pilatos \add (Pilate)\add* had judged him and wanted to release him.
\v 14 You disowned this man who was innocent and sinless, and instead you requested that a murderer be released to you.\x + \xo 3:14: \xt Mat 27:15-23; Mrk 15:6-14; Luk 23:13-23; Yhn 19:12-15.\x*
\v 15 However in doing so, you killed the creator of life, who \nd God\nd* then raised from the dead as we can attest.
\v 16 Now it’s \add our\add* faith in the \add authority\add* of \add Yeshua\add* that has strengthened \add the legs\add* of this man that you have seen and that you already knew—this \add authority\add* and faith that comes from \add Yeshua\add* completely healed this man right in front of you all.
\p
\v 17 \add So\add* now, friends, I know that you did \add all\add* that in ignorance, just like your leaders,
\v 18 but \add actually\add* \nd God\nd* was fulfilling what he’d had written by the prophets, that the \nd messiah\nd* should suffer.
\v 19 Because of that, \add you all need to\add* repent and turn back \add to God\add* so that your sins can be erased,
\v 20 and then times of refreshing will come from the presence of \nd Yahweh\nd*, and so that he may send \nd Yeshua\nd* the \nd messiah\nd* who’s been appointed, back \add to save\add* you.
\v 21 \add For now\add* it is appropriate for heaven to receive him, until the times of restoration of everything, which \add again\add* \nd God\nd* has had written by the faithful prophets.
\v 22 Indeed Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* said, ‘Our \nd God Yahweh\nd* will raise up a prophet for you that’s like one of your own brothers. You must listen to everything he says—whatever he tells you.\x + \xo 3:22: \xt Deu 18:15,18 (LXX).\x*
\v 23 Everyone who doesn’t listen to that prophet will end up totally destroyed and not part of \add God’s\add* people.’\x + \xo 3:23: \xt Deu 18:19.\x*
\v 24 Similarly, the prophets from Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add* onwards spoke in turn, and when they spoke they foretold these \add current\add* days.
\v 25 And you are the descendants of those prophets, and part of the agreement between \nd God\nd* and your ancestors, when he told Avraham, ‘All the families in the world will be blessed by one of your \add descendants\add*.’\x + \xo 3:25: \xt Gen 22:18.\x*
\v 26 \nd God\nd* had raised up his servant and sent him to you \add Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* first, to bless you by turning each of you away from your own \add wicked behaviour\add*.”
\c 4
\s1 Kefa and Yohan are threatened by the religious leaders
\p
\v 1 While \add Kefa\add* \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* \add and Yohan\add* \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* were still talking to the people, the chief priests and a temple officer and some people from the Saddoukaios \add (Sadducee)\add* sect approached them
\v 2 because they were very upset about what they’d been teaching the people, including preaching that \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* could give life to the dead.
\v 3 \add So\add* they arrested the two, and as it was already evening, they put them in jail overnight.
\v 4 Despite this, many people who’d heard \add their\add* message trusted \add in Yeshua\add* and the number of \add families\add* who were believers grew to around 5,000.
\p
\v 5 Then the next day, the religious leaders and the elders and the \add religious teachers\add* gathered together in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*
\v 6 along with those descended from high priests, including Hanan \add (Annas; Grk. Hannas)\add* the current high priest and Kayafa \add (Caiaphas; Grk. Kaiafas)\add* and Yohan and Alexandros \add (Alexander)\add*.
\v 7 After \add having Kefa and Yohan brought in and\add* standing them in the middle, they began to ask, “Under what authority did you do this?”
\p
\v 8 Then Kefa, having been filled \nd with the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*, said to them, “Rulers of the people and elders,
\v 9 if we’re being questioned today about a good deed done to a man who couldn’t walk and which resulted in his healing,
\v 10 then you all need to know, and the people of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* should also know, that it was done under the \add authority and name\add* \nd of Yeshua the messiah\nd*. He was the man from Nazaret that you executed on a pole, but who \nd God\nd* \add brought back to life\add*. It was because of his \add authority\add* that this man can now stand before you without deformity.
\v 11 This \add Yeshua\add* is:
\q1 ‘the stone which was rejected by you builders,
\q1 has now become the \add vital\add* cornerstone.’\x + \xo 4:11: \xt Psa 118:22.\x*
\m
\v 12 We can’t be saved by anyone else, because there’s no other \add name or authority\add* that’s been given in the entire world under which people can be saved.
\p
\v 13 The assembled leaders were amazed when they saw the boldness of Kefa and Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* who they knew to be illiterate, working-class men Then they recognised that they’d been with \nd Yeshua\nd*,
\v 14 but since the man who was no longer deformed was standing there with them, there was nothing that they could \add really\add* accuse them of.
\v 15 So they had them removed from the room, and they asked each other,
\v 16 “What should we do with these fellows? \add By now\add* everyone in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* has heard about the miracle they did, and we have no way to rebuff it.
\v 17 However, we have to stop it spreading even further, so maybe we should ban them from speaking about this name to anyone.”
\v 18 \add So\add* they called them back in and decreed that they weren’t allowed to preach or teach under the name \nd of Yeshua\nd* anymore.
\p
\v 19 But Kefa and Yohan answered, “You judge \add for yourselves\add* whether it’s right in \nd God’s\nd* eyes to listen to you rather than to \add him\add*?
\v 20 As for us, we’re not able to \add be silent\add* about what we saw and heard.”
\v 21 \add So\add* after making some further threats, the leaders sent them off. They hadn’t been able to find anything to punish them for, because the people were all praising \nd God\nd* for what had happened
\v 22 \add and\add* because the man who’d miraculously recovered was over forty years old.
\s1 The believers ask God for power
\p
\v 23 After \add Kefa\add* \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* \add and Yohan\add* \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* had been released, they went to their own \add group\add* and told them what the chief priests and elders had said.
\v 24 When they all heard this, they began to pray out loud to \nd God\nd*, saying, “Yahweh, you created heaven and earth and the sea, and everything in them.\x + \xo 4:24: \xt Exo 20:11; Neh 9:6; Psa 146:6.\x*
\v 25 You spoke \add long ago\add* by \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* through our ancestor who was your servant, David, when you said,
\q1 ‘Why are the other nations angry and why have \add their\add* peoples tried new \add ideas\add*?”
\q1
\v 26 The earthly kings and rulers stood and worked together against \nd Yahweh\nd* and against his \nd messiah\nd*.’\x + \xo 4:25-26: \xt Psa 2:1-2 (LXX).\x*
\m
\v 27 And indeed Herodes \add (Herod)\add*\x + \xo 4:27: \xt Luk 23:7-11.\x* and Pontios Pilatos \add (Pilate)\add*\x + \xo 4:27: \xt Mat 27:1-2; Mrk 15:1; Luk 23:1; Yhn 18:28-29.\x* met with the pagan \add Romaios\add* and the peoples of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* to conspire against your holy servant \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* who you chose.
\v 28 Then they did exactly what your \add power\add* and plan had already decided would happen.
\v 29 \add So\add* now, \nd Yahweh\nd*, see their threats and help us, your slaves, to be able to proclaim your message with boldness.
\v 30 Let us experience your \add power\add* to heal and do miracles and things that confirm the scriptures by means of the \add name and authority\add* of your holy servant, \nd Yeshua\nd*.”
\p
\v 31 As they finished their requests, the place where they had met was shaken and all of them were filled with the holy \nd spirit\nd*, and they \add went out and\add* boldly proclaimed \nd God’s\nd* message.
\s1 The believers share their wealth and possessions
\p
\v 32 And so the group of believers were united in their will and attitude. No one claimed anything to be their own, but they shared everything they had.\x + \xo 4:32: \xt Acts 2:44-45.\x*
\v 33 The missionaries of the \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, experienced great power and proclaimed the truth about how he \add came back to life\add*, and \add God’s\add* favour was with them.
\v 34 None of them were lacking anything, because any of them who owned properties or houses would sell them and donate the proceeds.
\v 35 They would lay them down \add in front of\add* the missionaries, and from there it was distributed around according to what \add needs\add* each person had.
\p
\v 36 Yosef \add (Joseph)\add* (a Levite born in Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add* who was nicknamed by the missionaries as ‘Barnabas’, meaning ‘Encourager’) \add was one who\add*
\v 37 owned a field which he sold, and brought the money and put it down \add in front of\add* the missionaries.
\c 5
\s1 The death of Hananyah and Sapfira
\p
\v 1 But in contrast, a couple named Hananyah \add (Ananias; Grk. Hananias)\add* and Sapfira sold a property
\v 2 but agreed together to hide some of the proceeds. So \add Hananyah\add* brought a smaller amount and put it down \add in front of\add* the missionaries.
\v 3 Then Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* said, “Hananyah, why has Satan filled your \add mind\add* such that you would lie to the holy \nd spirit\nd* and keep aside some of the proceeds of the property?
\v 4 Wasn’t it yours before it was sold? And even when it was sold, the money was still yours. Why did you decide to handle it like this? It wasn’t people that you’re lying to, but \nd God\nd*.”
\v 5 When Hananyah heard that, he fell down and stopped breathing. Everyone who heard about it felt very afraid,
\v 6 and the young men there stood up and covered his \add body\add*, carried it out, and buried him.
\p
\v 7 Three hours later, \add Hananyah’\add* wife came in, not knowing anything about what had happened.
\v 8 Kefa checked, “Is it true that you guys sold the property for such-and-such an amount?”
\p “Yes, for that amount,” she replied.
\p
\v 9 But Kefa said to her, “Why did the two of you agree to test \nd Yahweh’s spirit\nd*? The guys who just buried your husband are just out the door, and they’ll carry you out as well.”
\v 10 \add Sapfira\add* immediately fell down \add in front of\add* him and stopped breathing. Then the young men came in and found her dead, so they took \add her body out\add* and buried her with her husband.
\v 11 \add So\add* the entire assembly of believers became very afraid, along with everyone else who heard about these events.
\s1 Ongoing miracles and healings
\p
\v 12 The missionaries went on to do many miracles and amazing things, and all the people who gathered in Shelomoh’s Porch had a united vision,
\v 13 but no one else dared to join them. However, the people were talking positively about them
\v 14 and more were becoming believers in the \nd master\nd*—big numbers of both men and women.
\v 15 Some would even bring those \add who were\add* sick out to the side of the \add road\add*, lying them on beds and mats waiting for Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* to come past so that \add even\add* \add his\add* shadow might touch them.
\v 16 More crowds were coming from the towns around Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, bringing those \add who were\add* sick and \add those\add* tormented by demons, and they were all getting healed.
\s1 The preachers get arrested but released again
\p
\v 17 But the chief priest and his colleagues who were members of the sect of Saddoukaios \add (Sadducees)\add* were filled with jealousy and \add took action\add*
\v 18 by having the missionaries arrested and thrown into the public jail.
\v 19 But one \nd of Yahweh’s\nd* \add messengers\add* opened the prison doors during the night and led them out \add again\add*, saying,
\v 20 “Now go and stand in the temple and keep telling the people all the teaching about \add new\add* life.”
\v 21 \add So\add* at dawn, having \add taken notice\add* of the messenger, they went into the temple and started teaching \add the people there again\add*.
\p When the chief priest arrived with his companions, they called together the \add religious\add* council and also the \add political\add* council of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, then called for the missionaries to be brought in from the prison.
\v 22 But when their attendants got to the jail, they weren’t there, so they returned to the gathering and told them,
\v 23 “We found the prison all locked up with the guards in their positions at the doors, but when they opened it up, there was no one inside!”
\v 24 When they heard this, the officer of the temple and the chief priests were totally puzzled as to what could have happened.
\v 25 Just then someone came in and told them, “Hey, the men that you put in prison are in the temple—standing there teaching the people.”
\v 26 So the \add temple\add* officer took the attendants and went and got them, but didn’t use force because they were afraid that the people might start throwing rocks at them.
\p
\v 27 \add So\add* they brought them in and stood them in front of the council, where the chief priest addressed them,
\v 28 “We officially commanded you all not to be teaching in this \add name and authority\add*. But see here, now you’ve filled Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* with your teaching, and what’s more, you’re blaming us for the death of that man.”\x + \xo 5:28: \xt Mat 27:25.\x*
\v 29 But Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* and the other missionaries answered, “It’s better \nd to\nd* obey \nd God\nd* than people.
\v 30 The \nd god\nd* of our ancestors \add brought \+nd Yeshua\+nd*\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \add back to life\add* after you all handed him over to be hung up on a pole.
\v 31 But \nd God\nd* honoured this way-maker and saviour by placing him in the \add place of honour\add*, to \add encourage\add* Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* to \add repent\add* and \add then\add* to \add forgive\add* \add their\add* sins.
\v 32 We are witnesses of these messages, along with the holy \nd spirit\nd* who \nd God\nd* gave to the ones who obey him.”
\p
\v 33 When the gathered councils heard this, they were enraged and wanted to kill them,
\v 34 but a certain member of the Farisaios party named Gamliel stood up in the council chamber. He was a law teacher respected by all the community, and he ordered the \add guards\add* to take them outside for a while,
\v 35 then he said, “Fellow Israelis, think carefully about what you’re planning to do with these men.
\v 36 A while back, a man called Theudas became prominent, saying that he was someone important. \add Eventually\add* about four hundred others joined him, but he was killed and those who’d followed him were dispersed and it all came to nothing.
\v 37 A bit later on, a Galili man named Yehudah \add (Judas; Grk. Yudas)\add* became prominent at the time of the census and he attracted people to him. But he also died, and all those who’d been persuaded by him ended up being scattered.
\v 38 \add So\add* here’s my advice to you all: pull back from \add killing\add* these men and allow them \add to continue\add*, because if this teaching and goings on is man-made, it’ll be overthrown,
\v 39 but if it’s from \nd God\nd*, you all won’t be able to bring them down in case you find yourselves opposing God.”
\p So they were persuaded by \add Gamliel\add*,
\v 40 and after calling in the missionaries and having them beaten, they commanded them not to speak using the \add name and authority\add* \nd of Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* and then sent them out.
\v 41 So the missionaries left the \add councils\add*, very excited that they were considered worthy to be disgraced because of \add Yeshua\add*.
\v 42 After that, they never stopped teaching and preaching the good message about \nd Yeshua the messiah\nd* in the temple and in various homes.
\c 6
\s1 Seven administrators appointed
\p
\v 1 However around that time when the number of Yeshua-followers were growing, some of the Greek converts starting grumbling about the Jewish believers because the \add Greek\add* widows were being overlooked in the daily sharing \add of food and money\add*.
\v 2 \add So\add* the twelve \add missionaries\add* assembled the believers together and told them, “We’re not pleased about having to take time off \add sharing\add* the message \nd of God\nd* to be serving here at the \add distribution\add* tables.
\v 3 So brothers, look around you for seven men who are well known to be full \nd of\nd* \add God’s\add* \nd spirit\nd* and wisdom, so we can appoint them to be \add in charge of\add* this part of the work.
\v 4 Then we \add ourselves\add* can continue to pray and share the message.”
\p
\v 5 This idea pleased the group, so they went ahead and chose Stefanos (a man full of faith and \nd of the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*), Filippos \add (Philip)\add*, Prokoros, Nikanor, Timon, Parmenas, and Nikolaos (a non-Jew from Antiokeia who had previously converted to Youdaismos).
\v 6 They stood these seven up \add in front of\add* the missionaries, who prayed and then placed their hands on them.
\p
\v 7 \nd God’s\nd* message continued spreading and the number of followers in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* was rapidly increasing, and a large number of the priests became believers.
\s1 Stefanos gets arrested after teaching and doing miracles
\p
\v 8 Now Stefanos \add (one of the seven)\add* was full of grace and power and was doing miracles and amazing demonstrations of God’s power among the people.
\v 9 But a group from the Freedmen Synagogue as well as \add Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* from Kurainai \add (Cyrene)\add*, Alexandria, Kilikia, and Asia \add Minor\add* rose up and started arguing against Stefanos,
\v 10 but they weren’t able to refute the wisdom that he spoke \add with the help of\add* the pure \nd spirit\nd*.
\v 11 So they got some men \add to falsely accuse Stefanos\add* saying, “We heard him denigrating Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* and \nd God\nd*.”
\v 12 \add So\add* they stirred up the people and the elders and the law teachers and so they approached \add Stefanos\add* and arrested him and brought him to the council \add chambers\add*.
\v 13 There they produced the false witnesses saying, “This man won’t stop spreading messages against this holy \add temple\add* and against the law
\v 14 because we heard him saying that that \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* from Nazaret will be tearing down this building and will be changing the traditions that Mosheh gave to us.”
\v 15 \add So\add* then the council members looked across at \add Stefanos\add* and saw that his face \add was shining\add* like that of one \add of\add* \add God’s\add* \add messengers\add*.
\c 7
\s1 Stefanos’s history discourse and accusations
\p
\v 1 Then the chief priest asked \add Stefanos\add*, “Is all this true?”
\p
\v 2 And he replied, “My brothers and fathers, please listen. Our honoured \nd God\nd* was seen by our ancestor Avraham in Mesopotamia before he moved to Haran
\v 3 and he told him, ‘Leave your land and your relatives, and go to the place that I will show you.’\x + \xo 7:2-3: \xt Gen 12:1.\x*
\v 4 So he left Kasdim and moved to Haran,\x + \xo 7:4: \xt Gen 11:31.\x* and then after his father died, he moved again to this land where \add we\add* now live.\x + \xo 7:4: \xt Gen 12:4.\x*
\v 5 \add Avraham\add* hadn’t received any land here as an inheritance, in fact he’d never been near this land and nor did he have any \add children\add*, yet \add God\add* promised to give this \add entire\add* country to him and his descendants.\x + \xo 7:5: \xt Gen 12:7; 13:15; 15:18; 17:8.\x*
\v 6 And yet \nd God\nd* \add also\add* told him that his descendants would end up staying in another country where they would be enslaved and mistreated for four hundred years.
\v 7 However \nd God\nd* promised to punish the nation that enslaved them, and told him that in the end they would leave that place and serve him here in this country.\x + \xo 7:6-7: \xt Gen 15:13-14.\x*\x + \xo 7:7: \xt Exo 3:12.\x*
\v 8 And he contracted with \add Avraham\add* about being circumcised,\x + \xo 7:8: \xt Gen 17:10-14.\x* and so when \add Avraham\add* had Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add*, he circumcised him on the eighth day,\x + \xo 7:8: \xt Gen 21:2-4.\x* and then Yitshak went on to have Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*,\x + \xo 7:8: \xt Gen 25:26.\x* and Ya’akov to have the \add twelve\add* tribal leaders.\x + \xo 7:8: \xt Gen 29:31–35:18.\x*
\p
\v 9 Those brothers went on to become jealous of Yosef \add (Joseph)\add*\x + \xo 7:9: \xt Gen 37:11.\x* and sent him back into Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add* \add as a slave\add*,\x + \xo 7:9: \xt Gen 37:28.\x* but \nd God\nd* was with him\x + \xo 7:9: \xt Gen 39:2,21.\x*
\v 10 and rescued him out of all his troubles there. Then \add God\add* gave him wisdom and caused him to gain the favour of \add Par’oh\add* \add (Pharaoh)\add*, the king of Mitsrayim, who then appointed him as the ruler of all Mitsrayim and over Par’oh’s own household.\x + \xo 7:10: \xt Gen 41:39-41.\x*
\v 11 Then a famine hit all Mitsrayim and Kena’an \add (Canaan)\add* causing great distress and our ancestors were unable to find enough food.\x + \xo 7:11: \xt Gen 42:1-2.\x*
\v 12 But Ya’akov heard that grain was available in Mitsrayim, so first, he sent off our tribal leaders.
\v 13 On \add their\add* second visit, Yosef revealed himself to them,\x + \xo 7:13: \xt Gen 45:1.\x* and so Par’oh \add became aware\add* of Yosef’s heritage.\x + \xo 7:13: \xt Gen 45:16.\x*
\v 14 Then Yosef sent for his father Ya’akov and all the extended family\x + \xo 7:14: \xt Gen 45:9-10,17-18.\x*—some seventy-five of them.\x + \xo 7:14: \xt Gen 46:27 (LXX).\x*
\v 15 \add So\add* Ya’akov went to Mitsrayim,\x + \xo 7:15: \xt Gen 46:1-7.\x* where he and the \add twelve\add* tribal leaders \add eventually\add* died,\x + \xo 7:15: \xt Gen 49:33.\x*
\v 16 although their bodies were moved to Shekem where they were put into the tomb that Avraham had bought from the sons of Hamor.\x + \xo 7:16: \xt Gen 23:3-16; 33:19; 50:7-13; Josh 24:32.\x*
\p
\v 17 But as the time limit promised to Avraham by \nd God\nd* was getting closer, \add our\add* people multiplied in Mitsrayim\x + \xo 7:17-18: \xt Exo 1:7-8.\x*
\v 18 until another Par’oh ended up ruling \add there\add* who had never known Yosef.
\v 19 He took advantage of us Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* and mistreated our ancestors,\x + \xo 7:19: \xt Exo 1:10-11.\x* even forcing them apart from their own babies so they wouldn’t live.\x + \xo 7:19: \xt Exo 1:22.\x*
\v 20 Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* was born during that time and \nd God\nd* considered him beautiful. He spent three months in his \add own\add* father’s house\x + \xo 7:20: \xt Exo 2:2.\x*
\v 21 before being placed outside, from where Par’oh’s daughter took him and brought him up herself as her own son.\x + \xo 7:21: \xt Exo 2:3-10.\x*
\v 22 \add So\add* Mosheh was instructed in all the wisdom of the Mitsrim \add (Egyptians)\add*, and was a powerful speaker and project leader.
\p
\v 23 But when he hit forty, it entered his \add mind\add* to visit his relatives, the Israelis.\x + \xo 7:23-29: \xt Exo 2:11-15.\x*
\v 24 When he noticed one of them being mistreated, he retaliated and while defending the oppressed man, he struck \add and killed\add* the Mitsri \add (Egyptian)\add*.
\v 25 He thought \add his\add* relatives would understand that \nd God\nd* was bringing salvation to them via him, but they didn’t see it that way.
\v 26 \add So\add* on the following day when he saw two of them quarrelling, he tried to resolve the situation by saying, ‘Men, you’re all brothers. Why are you injuring each other?’
\v 27 But the bullying one pushed him away and said, ‘Who made you the judge over us?
\v 28 Do you want to kill me like you killed that Mitsri yesterday?’
\v 29 When he heard that, Mosheh fled \add Mitsrayim\add* \add (Egypt)\add* and became an exile in Midyan where he \add eventually\add* had two sons.\x + \xo 7:29: \xt Exo 18:3-4.\x*
\p
\v 30 After another forty years, he was in the wilderness around Mt. Sinay when he saw a messenger in the flame of a burning thorn bush.\x + \xo 7:30-34: \xt Exo 3:1-10.\x*
\v 31 Mosheh was amazed at the vision when he saw it, so he approached \add the bush\add* to look more closely when he heard Yahweh’s voice saying,
\v 32 ‘I am the \nd god\nd* of your ancestors—the \nd god\nd* of Avraham and of Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add* and of Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*.’ At that point, Mosheh started trembling and was scared to go any closer to look,
\v 33 but \nd Yahweh\nd* said, ‘Take off your \add sandals\add*, because the ground you’re standing on is dedicated to God.
\v 34 I have noticed the mistreatment of my people in Mitsrayim and heard their groaning and so I came down to rescue them, so now I’m going to send you \add back\add* to Mitsrayim.’
\p
\v 35 This was the same Mosheh that they had previously rejected and asked who had made \add him\add* ruler and judge over them.\x + \xo 7:35: \xt Exo 2:14.\x* \add Now\add* \nd God\nd* had indeed sent him as ruler and liberator by means of the messenger that he saw in the thorn bush.
\v 36 So \add eventually\add* \add Mosheh\add* led them out of Mitsrayim after doing miracles and signs of God’s power there,\x + \xo 7:36: \xt Exo 7:3.\x* and then also at the Red Sea\x + \xo 7:36: \xt Exo 14:21.\x* and during the forty years in the wilderness.\x + \xo 7:36: \xt Num 14:33.\x*
\v 37 This same Mosheh told Israelis that in the future \nd God\nd* would raise up another prophet like \add him\add* from among \add them\add*.\x + \xo 7:37: \xt Deu 18:15,18.\x*
\v 38 Now \add Mosheh\add* was the person that \add led\add* the assembly \add of people\add* in the wilderness, was spoken to by one \add of\add* \add God’s\add* \add messengers\add* on Mt. Sinay, and one of our ancestors that received living messages to pass on to \add us\add*.\x + \xo 7:38: \xt Exo 19:1–20:17; Deu 5:1-33.\x*
\p
\v 39 “Yet our ancestors didn’t want to obey that man, so they rejected him and started thinking about Mitsrayim again,
\v 40 saying to Aharon \add (Aaron)\add*, ‘Make some gods for us to lead us because we’ve got no idea what happened to that Mosheh who led us out of Mitsrayim.’\x + \xo 7:40: \xt Exo 32:1.\x*
\v 41 \add So\add* together they made a calf as an idol to bring \add sacrifices\add* to, and they were pleased with what they’d achieved.\x + \xo 7:41: \xt Exo 32:2-6.\x*
\v 42 But \nd God\nd* turned and allowed them to serve the heavenly \add armies\add*, just as the prophets wrote,
\q1 ‘It wasn’t me that you Israelis sacrificed to for those forty years in the wilderness.
\q1
\v 43 You turned to Molek’s tent and Remfan’s star as your \add gods\add* by making images of them to bow down to. So for that, I’ll send you all away into exile beyond Bavel \add (Babylon)\add*.’\x + \xo 7:42-43: \xt Amos 5:25-27 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 44 The tent of evidence was with our ancestors in the wilderness—made to the pattern that God had shown Mosheh.\x + \xo 7:44: \xt Exo 25:9,40.\x*
\v 45 Our ancestors then carried that tent into the land, having inherited it along with \nd Yehoshua\nd* \add (Joshua)\add* from the possession of the pagans which \nd God\nd* drove out ahead of them.\x + \xo 7:45: \xt Josh 3:14-17.\x* The tent remained there until the time of David
\v 46 who found favour with \nd God\nd* and requested to find a tent for the house of Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*,\x + \xo 7:46: \xt 2Sam 7:1-16; 1Ch 17:1-14.\x*
\v 47 but it was Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* who built the house for \add God\add* \add to reside in\add*.\x + \xo 7:47: \xt 1Ki 6:1-38; 2Ch 3:1-17.\x*
\p
\v 48 However the highest one doesn’t \add actually\add* live in structures made by people, but as the prophet wrote,
\q1
\v 49 ‘Heaven is my throne,
\q1 and the earth is where I place my feet.
\q1 How could you \add possibly\add* build a suitable house for me?
\q1
\v 50 Wasn’t it me that created everything?’\x + \xo 7:49-50: \xt Isa 66:1-2.\x*
\p
\v 51 You stiff-necked people with uncircumcised hearts and ears! You’re always opposing the holy \nd spirit\nd* just like your ancestors were.\x + \xo 7:51: \xt Isa 63:10.\x*
\v 52 Which one of the prophets wasn’t persecuted by your ancestors? Even the ones that foretold the coming of the righteous \add one\add* were killed off by them in just the same way that you yourselves became his betrayers and murderers!
\v 53 You’d received the law under the direction of messengers, and yet you didn’t obey it.”
\s1 Stefanos’s lynching and death
\p
\v 54 When they heard that, they were raging inside and grating their teeth at him.
\v 55 But being full \nd of the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*, \add Stefanos\add* stared up into the sky and was able to see \nd God’s\nd* splendour, as well as seeing \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* standing there on God’s right.
\v 56 He said, “I can look through the sky that’s opened up and can see the son of humanity standing at \nd God’s\nd* right side.”
\p
\v 57 At this point, they blocked their ears and rushed together to grab \add Stefanos\add*, yelling loudly.
\v 58 They drove him out of the city and then started throwing rocks at him. His accusers left their cloaks in the care of a young man named Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*.
\v 59 As Stefanos was being hit by the rocks, he called out, “\nd Master Yeshua\nd*, receive my spirit.”
\v 60 Then falling to his knees, just before he \add died\add* he called out loudly, “\nd Master\nd*, don’t hold this sin against them.”
\c 8
\p
\v 1 (And Sha’ul was agreeing that \add Stefanos\add* should be executed.)
\s1 Persecution of believers intensifies
\p That same day, intense persecution began against the assembly in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, and so the believers fled throughout the regions of Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* and Shomron \add (Samaria)\add*, except for the twelve who stayed.
\v 2 (Some godly men buried Stefanos with loud wailing.)
\p
\v 3 As for Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*, he started on destroying the assembly by entering people’s homes and dragging both men and women out and then off to prison.\x + \xo 8:3: \xt Acts 22:4-5; 26:9-11.\x*
\s1 Filippos preaches in Shomron/Samaria
\p
\v 4 However, now the people who had fled began to proclaim the good message to others in other places.
\v 5 Around the same time, Filippos \add (Philip)\add* went to a city in Shomron \add (Samaria)\add* and told them about the \nd messiah\nd*.
\v 6 Crowds gathered and listened carefully to Filippos and watched him do miracles—
\v 7 many demon-possessed people shouted loudly as the demons left them, and many who were paralysed or lame were healed
\v 8 \add so\add* there were a lot of happy people there.
\s1 Shim’on the magician seeks power
\p
\v 9 One man in the city had been practicing magic and amazing the people, and he claimed that he was someone great. \add His\add* name was Shim’on \add (Simon)\add*
\v 10 and the people there from the least to the most important said, “This power is from the great \nd god\nd*.”
\v 11 They gave him a lot of attention because he’d been amazing them with magic for quite a long time.
\v 12 However when Filippos \add (Philip)\add* started preaching the good message about \nd God’s\nd* kingdom and the \add name and authority\add* \nd of Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*, they believed the message, and many men and women asked to be immersed \add in water\add*.
\v 13 Shim’on himself also believed and was immersed, and then started accompanying Filippos around—marvelling as he observed the miracles and signs of God’s power.
\p
\v 14 When the missionaries in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* heard that many people from Shomron \add (Samaria)\add* had accepted \nd God’s\nd* message, they sent Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* and Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* out to them
\v 15 so then they prayed for them to receive \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*.
\v 16 (The holy spirit hadn’t fallen on any of them at that point, but they had only been immersed into the \add name and authority\add* of the \nd master Yeshua\nd*.)
\v 17 So \add Kefa and Yohan\add* placed their hands on the new believers and they received \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*.
\p
\v 18 When Shim’on the magician saw that they received the \nd spirit\nd* after having the hands of the missionaries placed on them, he offered money to \add Kefa and Yohan\add*, saying,
\v 19 “Give me this authority so that anyone who I place \em my\em* hands on will receive \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*.”
\p
\v 20 But Kefa responded, “May your money join you in your destruction because you thought you could buy \nd God’s\nd* gift with it.
\v 21 There’s no role for you here and no part for you in our work because your heart isn’t right with \nd God\nd*.
\v 22 You need to turn away from this evil \add desire\add* and beg the \nd master\nd* that maybe he’ll forgive you for your wrong \add intentions\add*
\v 23 because I perceive that you have a \add bitter attitude\add* and ungodly habits.
\p
\v 24 Shim’on answered, “Oh, please ask the \nd master\nd* on my behalf that none of what you mentioned will happen to me.”
\s1 Filippos immerses the Kushi official
\p
\v 25 After that, \add Kefa\add* \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* \add and Yohan\add* \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* continued to testify and proclaim the good message about the \nd master\nd* in many villages around Shomron \add (Samaria)\add* before they returned to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\p
\v 26 Then a messenger from \nd Yahweh\nd* spoke to Filippos \add (Philip)\add*, “Start walking and head south on the road that goes downhill from Yerushalem to Azah \add (Gaza)\add* through the wilderness.”
\v 27 \add So\add* he headed off, and \add on the road\add* he came across an important official of the Kushi \add (Ethiopian)\add* queen\f + \fr 8:27 \ft Some translations list the queen’s name as ‘Candace’, but that was the name of the series of queens, cf., Par’oh (Pharaoh) or Caesar which came to be used as a title for more than one person.\f* who was \add in charge of\add* her treasury. This man had been in Yerushalem to worship \add God\add*
\v 28 and was returning home in his chariot. As he rode, he was reading \add the scroll of\add* the prophet Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add*,
\v 29 and the \nd spirit\nd* told Filippos to approach him and stay by the chariot.
\v 30 \add So\add* Filippos ran up to the chariot and heard the man reading from the prophet Yeshayah and asked, “Do you know what it is that you’re reading about?”
\p
\v 31 “How could I know when I don’t have someone to guide me?” he answered, and so he invited Filippos to climb up and sit with him.
\v 32 Now the \add particular\add* passage that he was reading was this:
\q1 ‘He was led away like a sheep being led to the slaughterhouse,
\q1 and stayed as silent as a lamb being shorn
\q2 so he never opened his mouth.
\q1
\v 33 He was humiliated by a lack of justice.
\q1 Who can name his descendants,
\q2 because his life is being taken from the earth?’\x + \xo 8:32-33: \xt Isa 53:7-8 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 34 “Now tell me,” said the official, “who was Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* talking about? Was he talking about himself or someone else?”
\v 35 \add So\add* Filippos started with that passage and explained the good message about \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* to him.
\v 36 As they were going along the road, they came to some water, and the official asked, “Look, here’s some water. What would stop me from being immersed here?”
\p
\v 37 ◘
\v 38 \add So\add* he commanded \add the driver\add* to stop the chariot and they both climbed down, and Filippos \add (Philip)\add* immersed the official in the water.
\v 39 But as they came out of the water, \nd Yahweh’s spirit\nd* snatched Filippos away and so the official never saw him again, but he continued on his journey very happy.
\v 40 As for Filippos, he found himself at \add the town of\add* Azotos, and as he passed through \add the coastal towns\add* on the way to Kaisareia, he proclaimed the good message in each town.
\c 9
\s1 Yeshua talks to Sha’ul
\r (Acts 22:6-16; 26:12-18)
\p
\v 1 Meanwhile Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add* was still spouting off murderous \add threats\add* towards the followers of the \nd master\nd*. He went to the chief priest
\v 2 and obtained \add official\add* letters to enable him to enter the \add Jewish meeting halls\add* \add (Grk. synagogues)\add* in Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* so that if he found either male or female followers of Yeshua \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, he could arrest them and bring them to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* in chains.
\p
\v 3 On his journey as he approached Damesek, suddenly light from the sky flashed around him.
\v 4 He dropped to his knees\f + \fr 9:4 \ft It seems most likely that Paul and company were walking to Damascus (and not on horseback despite paintings by Caravaggio and others). The journey of somewhere around 300km would have taken around two weeks.\f* and heard a voice calling, “Sha’ul, Sha’ul, why are you persecuting me?”
\p
\v 5 “Who are you, master?” he asked.
\p And the voice replied, “I’m \nd Yeshua\nd*, the one that you’re persecuting.
\v 6 Now, stand up and go into the city, and you’ll be told there what you need to do.”
\p
\v 7 Meanwhile his travelling companions had stood there speechless, because they’d heard the voice but couldn’t see anyone.
\v 8 They helped Sha’ul up from the ground, but even when he opened his eyes, he couldn’t see anything. So they had to lead him into Damesek by hand.
\v 9 \add Sha’ul\add* stayed there for three days, unable to see and not eating or drinking anything.
\s1 Hananyah restores Sha’ul’s sight
\p
\v 10 Now there was a believer there in Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* named Hananyah \add (Ananias; Grk. Hananias)\add*, and the \nd master\nd* spoke to him in a vision, calling, “Hananyah.”
\p “I’m here, master,” he answered.
\p
\v 11 \add So\add* the \nd master told\nd* him, “Get up and go to Yehudah’ \add (Judas; Grk. Yudas)\add* house in Straight Street where you’ll find a man named Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add* from Tarsos. He’s praying right now
\v 12 and in \add his\add* vision, he saw a man named Hananyah who comes in and places his hands on him so that he can receive his sight back.”
\p
\v 13 But Hananyah resisted, “\nd Master\nd*, I’ve heard a lot about that man, including all the evil things he’s done to your dedicated \add people\add* in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*,
\v 14 and the reason he’s here in Damesek with the authority of the chief priests is to arrest everyone who trusts in you.”
\p
\v 15 But the \nd master\nd* said, “Go \add now\add*, because I’ve chosen that man to be a bearer of my \add name and authority\add* both in other countries, even to \add their\add* kings, and to the descendants of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*,
\v 16 because I’ll be showing him what he’s going to have to suffer for bearing my name.”
\p
\v 17 \add So\add* Hananyah left and went into Yehudah’ house where he placed his hands on \add Sha’ul\add* and said, “Brother Sha’ul, the \nd master\nd* has sent me here—\nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* the one you saw on the road when you were coming here—so that you’ll be able to see again and also be filled \nd with the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*.”
\v 18 Immediately something like scales fell off \add Sha’ul’s\add* eyes and he was able to see again, and he \add got up\add* and got immersed in water,
\v 19 before getting something to eat to renew his strength.
\s1 Sha’ul starts preaching in Damesek
\p Then \add Sha’ul\add* \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add* stayed several days with the believers in Damesek \add (Damascus)\add*.
\v 20 He went straight into the \add Jewish meeting halls\add* and started proclaiming that \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* is \nd God’s\nd* son.
\p
\v 21 The hearers were all amazed, saying, “Isn’t this the guy that persecuted the ones following that Yeshua in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, and even came to Damesek to arrest them here and take them back to the chief priests?”
\p
\v 22 But Sha’ul was growing stronger\f + \fr 9:22 \ft It’s not clear here whether it means that Saul was growing physically stronger, or that his preaching and debating skills were improving.\f* and he was confounding the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* living in Damesek as he taught them that \add Yeshua\add* is the \add promised\add* \nd messiah\nd*.
\p
\v 23 After several days, the religious Yehudim plotted together to kill him,\x + \xo 9:23-25: \xt 2Cor 11:32-33.\x*
\v 24 but Sha’ul \add found out\add* about their plan. Those \add Yehudim\add* were also watching the \add city\add* exits so they could kill him if he tried leaving,
\v 25 but his supporters took him to the \add city\add* wall at night time and lowered him down in a basket.
\s1 Sha’ul goes to Yerushalem but has to leave
\p
\v 26 When he got back to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, \add Sha’ul\add* \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add* attempted to join in with the believers there but they were all scared of him, not believing that he was a true follower of Yeshua \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*.
\v 27 However Barnabas took \add Sha’ul\add* and brought him to the twelve, and \add Sha’ul\add* told them how he had seen the \nd master\nd* on the road, and been spoken to by him, and how he had spoken boldly in Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* in the \add name and authority\add* \nd of Yeshua\nd*.
\v 28 \add So\add* \add Sha’ul\add* stayed connected with the believers, going in and out of Yerushalem and speaking boldly in the \add name and authority\add* of the \nd master\nd*.
\v 29 He also talked and debated with the Greek-speaking Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*, but they wanted to kill him,
\v 30 so when the brothers \add found out\add*, they took \add Sha’ul\add* down to Kaisareia \add on the coast\add*, and from there sent him \add on a ship\add* to Tarsos.
\p
\v 31 After that the \add assemblies\add*\f + \fr 9:31 \ft Lit. ‘assembly’ (singular). Jonathan Leeman in ‘One Assembly’ makes a good case that the force of κατά ‘throughout’ contributes to making the force of ἐκκλησία plural in this sentence (similar to Luke’s use in Luke 4:14, 23:5, Acts 9:42, 10:37).\f* of believers throughout Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* and Galil and Shomron \add (Samaria)\add* had peace and grew in their respect and service of the \nd master\nd*, as well as increasing in numbers through the encouragement of the holy \nd spirit\nd*.
\s1 Kefa heals Aineas
\p
\v 32 Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* travelled around the country and then went to visit the dedicated believers in Lod.
\v 33 He saw a paralysed man named Aineas who’d spent eight years lying on \add his\add* pallet,
\v 34 and said to him, “Aineas, \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd* is healing you. Stand up and pack up your bed.” And straight away Aineas stood up
\v 35 and all the people living in Lod and Sharon who saw him became followers of the \nd master\nd*.
\s1 Kefa brings Tabita back to life
\p
\v 36 Then in Yafo \add (Joppa)\add* there was a believer named Tabita (or Dorkas if translated) and she did a lot of good things as well as \add being generous to the poor\add*.
\v 37 Around that time she got sick and died. After washing her body, they placed it in an upper room,
\v 38 and since Yafo was near Lod and the believers had heard that Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* was there, they quickly sent two men to request, “Please come as soon as possible.”
\v 39 \add So\add* Kefa went with them, and when they arrived in Yafo they took him up to the room. All the widows stood there, sobbing and showing him various clothes that Tabita had made for them.
\v 40 But Kefa had them all removed from the room, and then kneeling down he prayed before turning back to the body and said, “Tabita, get up.” Then she opened her eyes, and seeing Kefa there she sat up.
\v 41 \add Kefa\add* helped her to stand up, then he called the believers and the widows, and presented the live \add Tabita\add* to them.
\v 42 News of this spread throughout Yafo and many people there believed in the \nd master\nd*.
\v 43 \add So\add* \add Kefa\add* stayed on \add there\add* for quite a few days, living with a tanner of hides named Shim’on \add (Simon)\add*.
\c 10
\s1 Kefa and Kornelios each hear from God
\p
\v 1 Just up in Kaisareia there was a \add Romaios\add* army commander named Kornelios (leader of the ‘Italian Regiment’)
\v 2 who, along with all his household, worshipped God and obeyed his laws. He gave to the poor and regularly asked \nd God\nd* for help.
\v 3 One afternoon around 3pm he had a vision where he clearly saw one \nd of God’s\nd* \add messengers\add* come into the house and call, “Kornelios.”
\p
\v 4 \add Kornelios\add* was scared and looked intently and asked, “What is it, master?”
\p “Your prayers and your helping the poor rose up to \nd God’s\nd* notice and attention,” he said.
\v 5 “Now, send some messengers to Yafo \add (Joppa)\add* to go and find a man there named Shim’on \add (Simon)\add* Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add*.
\v 6 He’s staying with Shim’on the tanner whose house is by the sea.”
\v 7 \add So\add* as soon as the messenger left, \add Kornelios\add* called two of \add his\add* house servants and a godly soldier from his bodyguard,
\v 8 and after explaining the task to them, he sent them off to Yafo.
\p
\v 9 The next day as they were approaching the town, Kefa went up onto the \add flat\add* roof of the house around midday to pray.
\v 10 But he felt hungry and wanted something to eat, but while it was still being prepared he fell into a trance
\v 11 and saw the sky open up. Something like a sheet was being lowered down to the earth by the four corners
\v 12 and it held all kinds of animals and reptiles, as well as birds.
\v 13 Then he heard a voice saying, “Get up Kefa, and slaughter these and eat them.”
\p
\v 14 “I can’t, \nd master\nd*,” Kefa responded, “because I’ve never eaten anything that’s forbidden.”
\p
\v 15 But the voice spoke a second time, “Don’t you be treating anything as unclean that \nd God\nd* has made clean.”
\v 16 This vision happened three times and then the sheet thing was lifted back up into the sky.
\p
\v 17 Now Kefa was still there wondering what the vision was all about when the men sent by Kornelios had tracked down the tanner’s house and arrived outside.
\v 18 They called out to ask if Shim’on \add (Simon)\add* Kefa was staying there.
\p
\v 19 Even as Kefa still continued to ponder about the vision, the \nd spirit\nd* said, “Hey, there’s three men here \add looking for\add* you.
\v 20 So get up and go downstairs to them. Believe everything they say because I sent them here.”
\v 21 \add So\add* \add he\add* went down and asked the men, “I’m the one you’re \add looking for\add*, so tell me what you came here for.”
\p
\v 22 They answered, “We were sent by Kornelios who’s an army commander who worships \nd God\nd* and obeys his laws and has a good reputation with you Jewish people. A holy messenger told him to send for you to come to his house so they can listen to what you have to say.”
\v 23 So \add Kefa\add* invited them in to stay the night.
\p The next day, \add Kefa\add* and some of the believers from Yafo \add (Joppa)\add* started out with them
\v 24 and they arrived in Kaisareia the following day. Kornelios was expecting them and had also invited some relatives and close friends to join them.
\v 25 Kefa entered the house and when they met, Kornelios knelt down and worshipped him
\v 26 but Kefa pulled him up and said, “Stand up. I’m just a man like you.”
\v 27 After they greeted each other, \add Kefa\add* went into the main room and found that quite a few people had gathered there.
\v 28 Then he told them, “You all know that us Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* are forbidden by our laws to associate with or to enter the house of a non-Jew. However, \nd God\nd* showed me that it’s not right to treat other people as being lesser or undesirable.
\v 29 That’s why I came without any reservations when you sent for me. But I’m \add still\add* waiting to know what you all want from me?”
\p
\v 30 \add So\add* Kornelios answered, “I was praying in my house three days ago in the middle of the afternoon when I suddenly saw a man in shining clothes standing \add in front of\add* me
\v 31 and he said, ‘Your prayer has been heard, Kornelios, and \nd God\nd* has noticed your gifts to the poor.
\v 32 Now he wants you to send messengers to get Shim’on \add (Simon)\add*, the one called Kefa, from Yafo \add (Joppa)\add* where he’s living by the sea in Shim’on the tanner’s house.’
\v 33 I did that straight away and you kindly came here, so now we’re all here in \nd God’s\nd* \add sight\add* to \add find out\add* everything that the \nd master\nd* has commanded you to say.”
\s1 Kefa’s talk in Kaisareia
\p
\v 34 \add So\add* Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* spoke up and said, “Honestly, I’m still just realising that \nd God\nd* doesn’t favour only certain people,\x + \xo 10:34: \xt Deu 10:17.\x*
\v 35 but he accepts people from any nation as long as they honour and obey him.
\v 36 He sent us a message through the descendants of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*—a good message that teaches that we can have peace through \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*—everyone’s \nd master\nd*.
\v 37 And you all know the news that started in Galil and spread through all Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* after the time of Yohan’s \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* teaching and ministry of immersion—
\v 38 how God filled \nd Yeshua\nd* from Nazaret \add (Nazareth)\add* \nd with the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* and with power, and who then went around doing good and healing all those oppressed by the devil, because \nd God\nd* was with him.
\v 39 We are witnesses of all the things he did in the Jewish provinces and in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, and then how they executed him \add by nailing him to\add* a stake.\x + \xo 10:39: \xt Jhn 20:25.\x*
\v 40 Then \nd God\nd* \add brought him back to life\add* on the third day and sent him where he could be seen,
\v 41 not by all the people, but to the witnesses that \nd God\nd* had previously appointed—to us who ate with him and drank with him after he’d been raised from the dead.
\v 42 He commanded us to tell the people, and to testify that he is the one who \nd God\nd* has designated to be the judge of the living and the dead.
\v 43 \add The writings of\add* all the prophets point towards him, and everyone who believes \add that he’s God\add* can have their sins \add forgiven\add* by his \add authority\add*.”
\s1 God’s spirit enters non-Jews
\p
\v 44 While Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* was still speaking, the holy \nd spirit\nd* came down onto everyone who was listening.
\v 45 The Jewish believers who had come with Kefa were astonished that the gift of the holy \nd spirit\nd* had been poured out even on non-Jews
\v 46 as they heard them speaking in other languages and magnifying \nd God\nd*. Then Kefa said,
\v 47 “Surely no one could argue against them getting immersed in water now that they’ve received the holy \nd spirit\nd* just like we did?”
\v 48 \add So\add* with the \add authority\add* \nd of Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*, he commanded for them to be immersed \add in water\add*. Then they asked him to stay with them for a few more days.
\c 11
\s1 Kefa explains everything to those in Yerushalem
\p
\v 1 \add Soon\add* the missionaries and other believers in Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* heard that non-Jews had also accepted \nd God’s\nd* message,
\v 2 but when Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* went to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, the strict Jewish believers were upset with him,
\v 3 saying that he had gone into the home of \add uncircumcised\add* pagans and \add even\add* eaten with them.
\p
\v 4 But Kefa started explaining from the beginning,
\v 5 “I was staying in Yafo \add (Joppa)\add*, and as I was praying I saw a vision of an object like a sheet coming down, as if it was being let down out of the sky by \add its\add* four corners. As it came down to me
\v 6 I looked at it and saw animals, even wild animals, and reptiles and birds.
\v 7 I also heard a voice telling me, ‘Kefa, get up and slaughter these and eat them.’
\v 8 But I said, ‘\nd Master\nd*, I can’t because I’ve never eaten anything that’s forbidden.’
\v 9 But the voice from the sky spoke again, ‘Don’t you be treating anything as unclean that \nd God\nd* has made clean.’
\v 10 This happened three times, and then it was pulled up again into the sky.
\v 11 Next minute there were three men at the house where I was staying \add saying\add* that they had been sent from Kaisareia to fetch me,
\v 12 and the \nd spirit\nd* told me to go with them without questioning anything they said. These six other believers went with me, and we arrived at the house of the man \add who had sent the three\add*.
\v 13 He told us how he’d seen one \add of\add* \add God’s\add* \add messengers\add* standing there in his house and who’d told him, ‘Send \add someone\add* to Yafo to fetch Shim’on \add (Simon)\add*, the one called Kefa,
\v 14 and he will explain to you how you and all your household can be saved.’
\v 15 Then when I started speaking to them, the holy \nd spirit\nd* came down on them just like it had happened to us previously.
\v 16 That reminded me of the words of the \nd master\nd* when he told us, ‘Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* immersed people in water, but you all will be immersed in \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*.
\v 17 Therefore if \nd God\nd* gave the same gift to them that he’d given to us when we’d believed in the \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*, what \add authority\add* did I have to contradict \nd God\nd*?’ ”
\p
\v 18 When the group heard all this, they had no objections and they praised \nd God\nd*, saying, “It seems that \nd God\nd* has also allowed the pagans to \add repent\add* and \add live\add*.”
\s1 Many in Antiokeia become believers
\p
\v 19 Over time, due to the persecution that began with Stefanos’s killing, the ones who had moved away went as far as Foinikai, Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add*, and Antiokeia, but they only shared Yeshua’s \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* teaching with other Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*.
\v 20 However, some of the believers were from Kupros and Kurainai \add (Cyrene)\add*, and when they got to Antiokeia they proclaimed the good news from the \nd master Yeshua\nd* to the Hellains \add (Greeks)\add*.
\v 21 \nd Yahweh\nd* caused their efforts to prosper, so a large number of Hellains believed and \add decided to follow\add* the \nd master\nd*.
\p
\v 22 News about this reached the assembly in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, so they sent Barnabas to Antiokeia.
\v 23 When he arrived and saw how \nd God’s\nd* grace \add was spreading\add*, he was elated and encouraged them all to earnestly continue to follow the \nd master\nd*.
\v 24 \add Barnabas\add* was good man and full \nd of the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* and full of faith \add in God\add*, and a large number of people \add decided to follow\add* Yeshua.
\p
\v 25 \add So\add* \add Barnabas\add* went to Tarsos to look for Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*,
\v 26 and when he found him, he brought him back to Antiokeia. Then for a whole year they met with the assembly and \add regularly\add* taught a large crowd. It was there in Antiokeia that the believers were first called Christians.
\p
\v 27 In those days, some prophets from Yerushalem went to Antiokeia,
\v 28 and one of them named Agabos when he was prompted by the \nd spirit\nd*, stood up and predicted that the world would experience a severe famine (and this happened \add later\add* during the reign of Klaudios).
\v 29 The believers decided to send assistance to their \add fellow believers\add* who lived in Yehudah \add (Judea)\add*—each one according to his means—
\v 30 and so they did, sending their contributions with Barnabas and Sha’ul to take to the elders.
\c 12
\s1 Persecution of believers restarts
\p
\v 1 Around that time, King Herodes \add (Herod)\add* \add Agrippas\add* used his \add power\add* to mistreat some of the believers from the assembly,
\v 2 and he had Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add*, the brother of Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add*, executed by the sword.
\v 3 Then when he saw that it pleased the Jewish religious leaders, he went on to seize Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* during the \it Flat Bread Celebration\it*.
\v 4 After being arrested, \add Kefa\add* was put in prison where he was guarded by four squads of four soldiers each. After the Passover Feast, \add Herodes\add* planned to display \add Kefa\add* to the people,
\v 5 but while \add he\add* was being held in prison, those in the assembly were earnestly asking \nd God\nd* to help him.
\s1 A messenger leads Kefa out of prison
\p
\v 6 Herodes \add (Herod)\add* planned to send for \add Kefa\add* \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* later that evening, but meanwhile Kefa had fallen asleep chained with two chains between two soldiers. There were also prison guards at the door
\v 7 but one \nd of Yahweh’s\nd* messengers \add suddenly appeared\add* standing by Kefa and light radiated through the prison cell. The messenger poked Kefa in the side to wake him up, saying, “\add Quickly\add*, get up!” The chains fell off his wrists
\v 8 and the messenger said, “Get dressed and put on your sandals,” so he did. Then the messenger said, “\add Now\add* put on your coat and follow me.”
\v 9 \add So\add* \add Kefa\add* followed the messenger out of the cell, not \add really\add* knowing if it was real or if he was \add still\add* dreaming.
\v 10 They went past the first guard and then the second, and then came to the iron gate which led out of the prison into the city. The gate opened by itself and they went out onto the street, then at the end of the street the messenger suddenly left him.
\p
\v 11 By now Kefa was fully awake and said to himself, “Now I know that the \nd master\nd* really did send his messenger to rescue me from Herodes and from the plans of the Jewish leaders.”
\p
\v 12 Now that he realised this, he went to Miryam’s \add (Grk. Maria/Mariam)\add* house (the mother of Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* Markos \add / John Mark\add*) where many believers had met to pray.
\v 13 He knocked at the door in the fence and a servant girl named Rode came out to see who it was.
\v 14 But when she recognised Kefa’s voice, she was so excited that she forgot about opening the door, but ran back inside to tell everyone that Kefa was standing out there.
\v 15 They told her that \add she\add* must be raving mad, but she insisted it was true, but they said that it must be his messenger.
\p
\v 16 Meanwhile Kefa continued knocking, and when they \add eventually\add* opened the outer door, they saw that it was him and they were amazed.
\v 17 But \add Kefa\add* signalled them with his hand to be quiet, and then he explained how the \nd master\nd* had led him out of the prison, and instructed them, “Tell this to Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add* and the brothers.” Then he left and went off to another place.
\p
\v 18 Once daylight came, there was pandemonium among the soldiers \add in the prison as they tried to figure out\add* what had happened to Kefa.
\v 19 Then when \add King\add* Herodes sent for \add Kefa\add* and he wasn’t there, the prison guards were interrogated and then taken away to be executed.
\p After that, \add Herodes\add* \add (Herod)\add* left Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* and went up to Kaisareia to stay.
\s1 The death of Herodes
\p
\v 20 Now King \add Herodes\add* \add (Herod)\add* was disputing with the people of Tsor \add (Tyre)\add* and Tsidon \add (Sidon)\add*, and so they got together and persuaded Blastos, a close personal servant of the king, to be their spokesman in asking for peace because they were dependent on food from the king’s provinces.
\p
\v 21 On the agreed day, Herodes, dressed in his royal garments and seated on his throne, started to address them.
\v 22 The crowd shouted out, “This is the voice \nd of a god\nd*, not of a man.”
\v 23 Because he didn’t attribute the honour \nd to God\nd*, one of the \nd Yahweh’s\nd* \add messengers\add* immediately struck him down and he stopped breathing as a result of a worm infestation.
\p
\v 24 Meanwhile \nd God’s\nd* message continued to spread and multiply.
\p
\v 25 By then Barnabas and Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add* had completed their ministry and returned to\f + \fr 12:25 \ft TD:Some manuscripts have ‘from’ here because in the next few sentences it seems that they were returned to Antioch.\f* Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* taking Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* Markos (Yohan Markos) along with them.
\c 13
\s1 The spirit chooses Barnabas and Sha’ul
\p
\v 1 In the assembly at Antiokeia there were some prophets and teachers: Barnabas, Shim’on \add (Simeon; Grk. Sumeon)\add* (called Niger), Lukios from Kurainai \add (Cyrene)\add*, Menahem \add (Grk. Manaen)\add* (who grew up with Herodes \add (Herod)\add* the tetrarch), and Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*.
\v 2 One day when they were ministering to the \nd master\nd* and fasting, the holy \nd spirit\nd* said, Appoint Barnabas and Sha’ul to serve me and do the work that I have called them to.”
\v 3 So they fasted and prayed and placed their hands on the two, and then sent them off.
\s1 Barnabas and Sha’ul go to Kupros to preach
\p
\v 4 So \add Barnabas and Sha’ul\add* \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*, having been sent by the holy \nd spirit\nd*, went down to Seleukeia \add at the coast\add* and from there sailed on to \add the island of\add* Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add*.
\v 5 When they arrived in \add the town of\add* Salamis, they proclaimed \nd God’s\nd* message in the \add Jewish meeting halls\add*. They also had Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* as their assistant.
\s1 Sergios believes after Bar-Yeshua is blinded
\p
\v 6 They travelled around the island and when they got to Pafos, they met a Jewish man named Bar-Yeshua \add (Grk. Bariesus)\add*.\f + \fr 13:6 \ft The Greek form of his name was Elymas according to verse 8.\f* He was a sorcerer and false prophet
\v 7 who was with Sergios Paulos the proconsul, an intelligent man. \add Sergios\add* sent for Barnabas and Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add* because he wanted to hear \nd God’s\nd* message from them,
\v 8 but the sorcerer opposed them and tried to prevent Sergios from believing.
\v 9 So then Sha’ul (the \add Romaios\add* form of his name was Paulos), filled \nd with the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* looked intently at \add Bar-Yeshua\add*
\v 10 and said, “You’re full of all kinds of deceit and fraud you child of the devil. Will you never stop twisting the truth about following the \nd master\nd*?
\v 11 Well, look here! \nd Yahweh\nd* will extend his \add power\add* against you and blind you—you’ll go a season without seeing the sun!”
\p And immediately he could only see fog and darkness, and needed someone to guide him around by the hand.
\v 12 The proconsul believed when he saw what had happened, and was amazed as he learnt about the \nd master\nd*.
\s1 Paulos and Barnabas in Pisidia
\p
\v 13 After that, Paulos\f + \fr 13:13 \ft From this point on in the text, Paul is referred to by his Roman name as his ministry is largely outside of Yisrael. (In Yisrael he seemed to be better known as Saul, the Greek form of his Hebrew name Sha-ul.)\f* and his companions sailed from Pafos to Pergai in Pamfulia, but Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* Markos \add (John Mark)\add* left them and returned to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 14 \add Paulos and his companions\add*, went on from Pergai to Pisidian Antiokeia, where they entered the \add Jewish meeting hall\add* on the Rest Day and sat down \add with the congregation\add*.
\v 15 After the \add readings\add* from the Law and from the Prophets,\f + \fr 13:15 \ft The Hebrew Scriptures are divided into the Law, the Prophets, and the Writings.\f* the leaders sent a messenger over to them to ask, “Men, brothers, if you bring any message of encouragement for the congregation, please share it.”
\p
\v 16 \add So\add* Paulos stood up and, motioning with his hand, started speaking, “Fellow Israelis and everyone who reveres \nd God\nd*, listen to me.
\v 17 The \nd God\nd* of the people of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* chose our ancestors and helped the people to prosper when they were staying in Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add*. Then \add later\add* he showed his \add power\add* as he led them out
\v 18 and endured them in the wilderness for around forty years.
\v 19 Then having overthrown seven nations in Kena’an \add (Canaan)\add*,\x + \xo 13:19: \xt Deu 7:1.\x* he passed on that land as an inheritance\x + \xo 13:19: \xt Josh 14:1.\x*
\v 20 after around 450 years.
\p “Then he gave them judges through until the time of the prophet Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add*
\v 21 when they requested a king, and \nd God\nd* gave them Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*, the son of Kish from the tribe of Benyamin to reign for forty years.
\v 22 After \add Sha’ul\add* was removed, \add God\add* chose David to be their king—the man of whom he said, ‘I found David the son of Yishay \add (Jesse)\add*, a man who wants to obey me and do everything I ask.’\x + \xo 13:22: \xt 1Sam 13:14; Psa 89:20.\x*
\v 23 Then \add eventually\add* a descendant of \add David\add* arrived in Yisrael as promised—the saviour \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*.
\v 24 Before he arrived Yohan-the-immerser had publicly preached to all the people of Yisrael about \add being immersed\add* \add as a sign\add* of repentance.
\v 25 As Yohan’s \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* ministry was nearing an end, he asked, ‘Who do you suspect I am? I’m not the messiah, but he will come after me, and I’m not \add even\add* worthy to undo his \add sandals\add*.’
\p
\v 26 “Men, brothers, descendants of Avraham and others here who revere \nd God\nd*, the teaching about this salvation has been sent to us.
\v 27 The people of Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* and their \add religious\add* leaders didn’t recognise this \add Yeshua\add*, but by their \add judgement\add* of him they fulfilled the words of the prophets that are read every Rest Day.
\v 28 Despite not finding even one charge against him worthy of death, they requested Pilatos \add (Pilate)\add* to execute him
\v 29 and when they’d done all the things that \add the prophets\add* had written about him, they took his body down from the post \add he’d been nailed to\add*\x + \xo 13:29: \xt Jhn 20:25.\x* and placed it in a \add burial chamber\add*.
\v 30 But \nd God\nd* \add brought him back to life\add*
\v 31 and his followers who had come to Yerushalem from Galil saw him for several days and now testify about this to the people.
\v 32 \add So\add* now we’re preaching this good message to you that what God promised to \add our\add* ancestors
\v 33 has now been fulfilled for our descendants, now that \nd God\nd* \add brought \+nd Yeshua\+nd* back to life\add* just like it’s written in the second psalm:
\q1 ‘You are my son;
\q1 today I have borne you.’\x + \xo 13:33: \xt Psa 2:7.\x*
\m
\v 34 \add God\add* \add brought him back to life\add*, and he’s never going to return \add to the grave\add* to decay. As \add God\add* said,
\q1 ‘I will give David’s faithful devout \add things???\add* to you all.’\x + \xo 13:34: \xt Isa 55:3.\x*
\m
\v 35 So in another psalm \add God\add* said,
\q1 ‘You won’t allow your devout \add one\add* to experience decay.’\x + \xo 13:35: \xt Psa 16:10.\x*
\m
\v 36 \add This can’t be David\add*, because after he’d served his own generation as \nd God\nd* led him, he indeed \add died\add* and was buried with his ancestors and his body decayed.
\v 37 On the contrary, the one who \nd God\nd* raised from the dead never experienced decay.
\v 38 So brothers, let it be known to you all that \add your\add* sins \add can now be forgiven\add* through this \add Yeshua\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*—the law of Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* was never able to offer you that.
\v 39 Everyone who \add puts their trust in\add* \add Yeshua\add* is able to receive a clean record.
\v 40 But keep your eyes open in case something else the prophets wrote might \add come true\add*:
\q1
\v 41 ‘Watch you scoffers, and be amazed and die because I’m doing something special during your time.
\q1 Even if someone described it clearly to you all, you still might not believe it.’ ”\x + \xo 13:41: \xt Hab 1:5.\x*
\p
\v 42 As they left \add the meeting hall after the service\add*, the people invited them to speak \add again\add* the following Rest Day.
\v 43 But when they started walking away, many people followed after Paulos and Barnabas, who then addressed them and encouraged them to continue trusting in \nd God’s\nd* grace.
\p
\v 44 \add So\add* on the following Rest Day, nearly everyone in the city had gathered there to hear the message from \nd God\nd*.
\v 45 But the Jewish religious leaders became very jealous when they saw the crowds and claimed that Paulos’s words had slandered God.
\v 46 But Paulos and Barnabas responded boldly, “It was necessary for \nd God’s\nd* message to be explained \add in the meeting hall\add* to all of \add the Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* first. But since you are rejecting that teaching, you are all judging yourselves to be unworthy of \add living\add* forever, so instead, we will \add now\add* share it with the non-Jews.
\v 47 Because this is what the \nd master\nd* commanded us:
\q1 ‘I have chosen you to be a light for the pagans;
\q2 You will take salvation to the end of the earth.’\x + \xo 13:47: \xt Isa 49:6.\x*
\p
\v 48 When the non-Jews heard that, they were very happy and were praising the \nd master’s\nd* message, and as many as were appointed to \add receive\add* eternal life believed \add it\add*.
\p
\v 49 \add So\add* from there, the \nd master’s\nd* message spread throughout the entire region.
\v 50 But the Jewish religious leaders incited some prominent women from the meeting halls and some from the local city government, and they then stirred up persecution against Paulos and Barnabas and had them expelled out of their \add region\add*.
\v 51 So \add Paulos and Barnabas\add* just left that place to face their own consequences, and continued on to Ikonion.
\v 52 Meanwhile the believers \add back in Pisidian Antiokeia\add* were full of happiness and of \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*.
\c 14
\s1 Paulos and Barnabas in Ikonion
\p
\v 1 A similar thing happened in Ikonion. \add Paulos and Barnabas\add* went into the \add Jewish meeting hall\add* and spoke in such a way that a lot of both Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* and Hellains \add (Greeks)\add* believed.
\v 2 But those Yehudim who didn’t believe got the non-Jews emotionally stirred up to oppose the believers.
\v 3 So \add Paulos and Barnabas\add* ended up staying there for quite a while, and as they spoke boldly for the \nd master\nd*, he himself confirmed his grace by enabling the two of them to be able to do miracles and amazing things.
\v 4 Nevertheless, the people of the city were divided—some agreeing with the \add strict\add* Yehudim, and others supporting the missionaries.
\p
\v 5 At one point, both the non-Jews and the strict Yehudim and their leaders got worked up enough to come in order to attack them and throw rocks to kill them.
\v 6 However, \add Paulos and Barnabas\add* \add found out\add* about it and escaped to the Lukaonios cities of Lustra and Derbai and the surrounding region
\v 7 and preached the good message there.
\s1 A miracle in Lustra following by a stoning
\p
\v 8 In Lustra there was a man sitting there who had been unable to use his legs since birth.
\v 9 He was listening to Paulos speaking, when \add Paulos\add* looked straight at him and realised that the man had the faith to be healed.
\v 10 So in a loud voice, \add Paulos\add* said, “Stand upright on your feet.” And the man sprang up and was walking around.
\v 11 The \add crowd\add* of people who saw what Paulos had done began to shout out in Lukaonios, “These two look like men, but they’re really gods who have come down to us!”
\v 12 They called Barnabas ‘Zeus’ and \add called\add* Paulos ‘Hermas’ since he was the main speaker.
\v 13 And the priest of Zeus wanted to sacrifice with the crowds, so he brought bulls and garlands \add of flowers\add* to the city gates from \add the temple\add* on the path to the city.
\p
\v 14 But when the missionaries Barnabas and Paulos heard what was going on, they tore their clothes \add in dismay\add* as they rushed out into the crowd
\v 15 shouting, “Men, why are you doing all these \add things\add*? We are only humans just like all of you. We were bringing you the good message about abandoning these useless \add sacrifices\add* and turning to the living \nd God\nd* who made heaven and the earth and the sea and everything in them.\x + \xo 14:15: \xt Exo 20:11.\x*
\v 16 In previous generations he allowed all the nations to go their own different ways,
\v 17 but he never left himself without evidence of himself—sending good rains from heaven to give you all fruitful seasons with food to fill \add your stomachs\add* and happiness to fill your hearts.”
\v 18 By saying these \add things\add*, they were \add eventually\add* just able to restrain the people from sacrificing to them.
\p
\v 19 But some \add strict\add* Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* came over from \add Pisidian\add* Antiokeia and Ikonion and were able to persuade the crowds. They threw rocks at Paulos \add to kill him\add* and dragged his body outside the city \add gates\add* thinking that he was dead.
\v 20 But as the believers were standing around him, \add Paulos\add* got up and went back into the city, but he left \add again\add* with Barnabas the next day and went to Derbai.
\s1 Paulos and Barnabas return to the sending assembly in Antiokeia
\p
\v 21 They preached the good message in Derbai, and after giving further training to many of them, they returned to Lustra and Ikonion and Antiokeia.
\v 22 There they strengthened the faith of the believers—encouraging them to continue believing and to expect that it’s normal to endure a lot \nd of\nd* suffering on the path to \nd God’s\nd* kingdom.
\v 23 They appointed some elders for \add each\add* assembly, then they fasted and prayed for the elders—asking the \nd master\nd* that they believed in to \add look after\add* them.
\p
\v 24 After passing through Pisidia, they arrived at Pamfulia
\v 25 and after teaching the \add master’s\add* message in Pergai, they went down to \add the coastal city of\add* Atalia.
\v 26 From there, they sailed \add back\add* to \add Arami\add* \add (Syrian)\add* Antiokeia where the congregation had prayed for \nd God’s\nd* grace to be with them\x + \xo 14:26: \xt 13:3.\x* for the work they had just fulfilled.
\p
\v 27 \add So\add* when they arrived, they gathered the assembly together there and shared about what \nd God\nd* had done with them and how he had opened up the door of faith to the non-Jews.
\v 28 Then they stayed on with them there for quite a while.
\c 15
\s1 The decision from the Yerushalem elders
\p
\v 1 Then some \add people\add* came \add to Antiokeia\add* from Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* and were teaching the believers that according to Mosheh’s \add (Moses)\add* teachings, you can’t be saved unless you’ve been circumcised.
\v 2 This led to no end of argument and debate between them and Paulos and Barnabas until it was decided that \add the two of them\add* and some other believers should go to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* to ask the missionaries and the elders there about this issue.
\p
\v 3 So indeed the assembly sent the group on their way, and as they passed through Foinikai and Shomron \add (Samaria)\add*, they described how non-Jews had become believers and this news made the believers there very happy.
\p
\v 4 When they arrived in Yerushalem, they were welcomed by the assembly and the missionaries and the elders, and they explained how \nd God\nd* had used them.
\v 5 But some of the believers who were also members of the Farisaios party stood up and said, “It’s quite proper for them to be circumcised and to be commanded to keep the Jewish religious \add laws\add*.”
\p
\v 6 \add So\add* the missionaries and the elders met \add separately\add* in order to consider the matter further.
\v 7 After a lot of debate, Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* stood up and said, “Men, brothers, all of you here know that some time back, \nd God\nd* chose me to be a mouthpiece so that the non-Jews could hear the good message and believe.
\v 8 And \nd God\nd* who knows even people’s inner thoughts, gave them the same holy \nd spirit\nd* that he gave us,
\v 9 and when he purified their \add minds\add* by the faith, he didn’t do anything different for them than he did for us.
\v 10 So why are you all provoking \nd God\nd* by putting a load on the shoulders of these believers that neither we, nor our ancestors, were ever able to bear?
\v 11 But we ourselves have believed thanks to the grace of \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the master\nd*, and they can be saved in just the same way.”
\p
\v 12 The whole group sat in \add silence\add*, and then they listened to Barnabas and Paulos as they described everything that \nd God\nd* had done among the non-Jews through them, including miracles and amazing things.
\v 13 Then while they were still silent, Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add* responded, “Men, brothers, listen to me.
\v 14 Shim’on \add (Simon)\add* Kefa has told us how \nd God\nd* first intervened in order to select a people for himself from among the non-Jews.
\v 15 And this \add actually\add* agrees with the \add message\add* that the prophets recorded,
\q1
\v 16 ‘After these \add things\add* have happened I will return,
\q1 and I will rebuild the worship tent from David’s time that has collapsed,
\q1 Although it was overturned, I will rebuild and restore it,
\q1
\v 17 so that the remaining people will want to find the \nd master\nd*,
\q1 and also the non-Jews who cried out to me.
\q1 \nd Yahweh\nd* who’s doing these \add things\add* is saying this,
\q1
\v 18 and planned it this way from eternity.’\x + \xo 15:16-18: \xt Amos 9:11-12.\x*
\p
\v 19 Because of this, my ruling is not to be troubling the non-Jews who are turning back to \nd God\nd*,
\v 20 but to write to them to keep away from anything \add polluted\add* by idols, from sexual immorality, and from strangled animals and \add eating or drinking\add* blood.
\v 21 Mosheh’s \add (Moses)\add* writings have been preached in every city and are read in \add our\add* \add meeting halls\add* on every Rest Day.
\s1 The letter is carried to Antiokeia
\p
\v 22 So the missionaries and elders with all the assembly decided to choose some men to accompany Paulos and Barnabas to Antiokeia, so they chose Yehudah \add (Judas; Grk. Yudas)\add* (nicknamed Bar-Saba) and Silas who were leaders among the believers,
\v 23 This is what they wrote: This letter is from the missionaries and elders, \add your\add* brothers \add here in Yerushalem\add* \add (Jerusalem)\add* to our non-Jewish \add fellow believers\add* in Antiokeia and Aram \add (Syria)\add* and Kilikia.
\v 24 Since we heard that some people who weren’t sent by us came from here and disturbed you all with their upsetting messages,
\v 25 we unanimously agreed to select some men to accompany our dear \add friends\add* Barnabas and Paulos back.
\v 26 These are men who have dedicated their lives to our \nd master\nd*, \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*.
\v 27 So we’ve sent Yehudah and Silas and they’ll confirm in person what we are writing.
\v 28 It seemed right to the holy \nd spirit\nd* and to us to not burden you all with anything more than the following commands:
\v 29 you all must keep away from anything sacrificed to idols, from \add eating or drinking\add* blood or strangled animals, and from sexual immorality. You will do well if you avoid these things. Farewell.
\p
\v 30 So they were sent off and when they arrived at Antiokeia, they gathered the believers and handed across the letter,
\v 31 and when they read it, they were very happy with the encouraging contents.
\p
\v 32 Yehudah and Barnabas were both prophets, and said a lot to encourage them and strengthen \add their faith\add*.
\v 33 After they’d stayed there for a while, the believers sent them home carrying greetings from them to the assembly \add back in Yerushalem\add* \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\p
\v 34 ◘
\v 35 But Paulos and Barnabas stayed on there in Antiokeia, teaching them and preaching the good message along with many others.
\s1 Paulos and Barnabas go separate ways
\p
\v 36 After a few days, Paulos suggested to Barnabas, “Let’s go back and visit the believers in each city where we proclaimed the \nd master’s\nd* message, and see how they’re doing.”
\v 37 Barnabas agreed and wanted to take Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* Markos \add (John Mark)\add* along with them as well,
\v 38 but Paulos didn’t want to take him because he had left them in Pamfulia instead of continuing with them in \add their\add* work.
\v 39 And so it turned into such a disagreement that the two of them each went their different ways. Barnabas took Yohan Markos with him and sailed to Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add*
\v 40 while Paulos chose Silas and they left after being entrusted to the \nd master’s\nd* grace by the believers.
\v 41 They went through Aram \add (Syria)\add* and Kilikia, strengthening the assemblies there.
\c 16
\s1 Paulos and Silas also take Timotheos with them
\p
\v 1 Then \add Paulos\add* went to Derbai and then on to Lustra where a believer named Timotheos lived. His mother was a Jewish believer, but his father was Greek.
\v 2 The believers in Lustra and Ikonion spoke well of \add Timotheos\add*
\v 3 and Paulos wanted him to accompany them on his trip so he circumcised him \add in advance\add* because of the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* in that region—everyone knew that his father was a Greek.
\v 4 As they passed through various towns, they explained the decision of the missionaries and elders in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* about which commands they must adhere to.
\v 5 So the assemblies were strengthened in this way and \add their\add* \add numbers\add* were increasing each day.
\s1 Paulos’s vision in Troias
\p
\v 6 From there, they travelled through Frugia and the Galatia region, after the holy \nd spirit\nd* prevented them from proclaiming the message in Asia \add Minor\add*.
\v 7 When they got to Musia, they planned to go on into Bithunia, but \nd Yeshua’s \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* spirit\nd* didn’t let them,
\v 8 \add so\add* after leaving Musia they went to Troias.
\v 9 During the night, Paulos had a vision of a Makedonian man standing there and imploring him, “Come on to Makedonia and help us here.”
\v 10 \add So\add* as a result of the vision, we decided to go straight to Makedonia, concluding that \nd God\nd* had called us to share the good message with them.
\s1 Ludia’s enthusiasm at Filippou
\p
\v 11 From Troias we sailed directly to Samothrakai, and then on to Neapolis the following day.
\v 12 From there we continued on to Filippou \add (Philippi)\add* which is a \add Romaios\add* colony and the capital city of the Makedonia district where we stayed for several days.
\v 13 On the Rest Days, we went \add down\add* to the river outside the \add city\add* gate where we thought people might be praying, and we spoke to the women who had met there.
\v 14 One of them was Ludia from Thuateira who was a seller of purple goods\f + \fr 16:14 \ft It’s possible that Lydia dyed fabric and sold it, or that she actually made and sold the purple dye itself.\f* and although she already worshipped \nd God\nd*, the \nd master\nd* opened her \add mind\add* and she \add readily\add* accepted what Paulos was saying.
\v 15 \add So\add* then \add Ludia\add* and her household were all \add ceremonially\add* immersed, and then she requested, “If you all consider me to be faithful to the \nd master\nd*, come and stay at my place.” So she persuaded us.
\s1 Paulos and Silas get thrown into prison
\p
\v 16 One time as we were walking to the prayer meeting, we met a female slave who was possessed by a spirit that enabled her to tell fortunes, and thanks to that, to earn a lot of money for her masters.
\v 17 She walked along behind \add us\add* and shouted out, “These men are slaves \nd of\nd* the highest \nd god\nd* and they will be telling you all how to be saved.”
\v 18 After she’d done this for several days, Paulos was so frustrated that he turned around and spoke to the spirit \add in her\add*, “I command you by the \add authority\add* \nd of Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd* to leave her.”
\p
\v 19 But when her masters realised that their profitable business was about to \add come to an end\add*, they grabbed Paulos and Silas and dragged them to the authorities at the marketplace.
\v 20 They brought them forward to face the magistrates and said, “These Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* are disturbing \add the peace\add* in our city
\v 21 and teaching behaviour that we as Romaios can neither accept nor practice.”
\v 22 An \add angry\add* crowd gathered around and the magistrates ordered for \add Paulos and Silas\add* to have their robes torn off and then to be beaten with rods.
\v 23 They were severely beaten and then thrown into prison where the officer was commanded to keep them secure.
\v 24 Because of that, he threw them into the inner cell and locked their feet in stocks.
\p
\v 25 Around midnight Paulos and Silas were praying and singing praises \nd to God\nd*, and the other prisoners were listening to them.
\v 26 Suddenly there was a strong earthquake that shook even the foundations of the prison, and immediately all the prison doors opened up and the chains of all the prisoners came loose.
\v 27 The prison officer woke up, and when he saw that all the prison doors were open, he drew his sword to take his own life as he figured that all the prisoners must have escaped.
\v 28 But Paulos shouted out, “Don’t harm yourself! We’re all \add still\add* here!”
\p
\v 29 The officer called for torches, then rushed in and knelt down trembling \add in front of\add* Paulos and Silas.
\v 30 Then he escorted them out and asked, “Masters, what do I need to do to be saved?”
\p
\v 31 “Believe in the \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*,” they replied, “then you and your household can all be saved.”
\v 32 Then they explained the message about the \nd master\nd* to him and all those in his household.
\v 33 Even though it was still the middle of the night, he sponged their wounds, and then him and his household were all immersed straight away.
\v 34 He took \add Paulos and Silas\add* up into \add his\add* house and gave them a meal, and \add his\add* whole household were happy because they now believed \nd in God\nd*.
\p
\v 35 The next day the magistrates sent officials to tell the jailer to release \add Paulos and Silas\add*.
\p
\v 36 \add So\add* the officer told Paulos, “The magistrates \add sent these officials\add* to order that you two be released.”
\p
\v 37 But Paulos responded, “You mean that after publicly beating us Romaios men without a trial, they threw us into prison and now they want to just quietly release us.”
\p
\v 38 \add So\add* the officials reported this back to the magistrates, and they were quite afraid when they heard that \add Paulos and Silas\add* were Romaios \add citizens\add*.
\v 39 \add So\add* now they came to the prison in person, and appealed to them, and escorting them out of prison themselves, asked them to please leave the city.
\v 40 \add So\add* after \add Paulos and Silas\add* left the prison, they went to see Ludia and after encouraging the believers there, they left.
\c 17
\s1 Yason gets in trouble in Thessalonikai
\p
\v 1 After travelling through Amfipolis and Apollonia, \add Paulos and Silas\add* went to Thessalonikai where there was a \add Jewish meeting hall\add*.
\v 2 Paulos’s custom was to attend at the \add local\add* meeting hall on the Rest Day, so he did that, and for three weekends in a row he discussed the scriptures with them,
\v 3 showing them how that it \add had been written\add* that the \nd messiah\nd* would suffer and be killed and \add come back to life\add*, and then explaining, “This \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* that I’ve been telling you about is \nd the messiah\nd*.”
\v 4 Some of them were persuaded and joined with Paulos and Silas, along with a big group of godly Hellains \add (Greeks)\add* and quite a few prominent women.
\p
\v 5 But some of the other Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* were envious \add of them\add*, and enlisted some of the troublemakers from the marketplaces to form a mob. They stirred up all the people, and \add looking for\add* \add Paulos and Silas\add* so they could bring them out, arrived at the house of a man named Yason.
\v 6 But when they realised that \add Paulos and Silas\add* weren’t there, they dragged Yason and some other believers off to the city leaders, shouting, “Those two men who’ve gone around upsetting the whole world have \add now\add* come here as well
\v 7 and Yason has welcomed them \add into his house\add*. They rebel against Kaisar’s decrees and claim that \nd Yeshua\nd* is a rival king.”
\v 8 They stirred up the crowd and the city leaders with these accusations,
\v 9 \add so\add* the leaders collected a heavy fine from Yason and the others, and then released them.
\s1 Many new believers in Beroia
\p
\v 10 \add So\add* the believers immediately sent Paulos and Silas off during the night to go to Beroia. In Beroia they also attended at the \add Jewish meeting hall\add*
\v 11 and this group were more upper-class than the congregation in Thessalonikai. They eagerly accepted the message and examined the scriptures every day to check if what was being taught matched what was written there.
\v 12 Because it did, many of the people believed including quite a few of the prominent Hellains \add (Greeks)\add*.
\v 13 But when the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* from Thessalonikai \add found out\add* that \nd God’s\nd* message was also being proclaimed by Paulos there in Beroia, they \add soon\add* turned up to agitate and stir the crowds.
\v 14 \add So\add* the believers immediately sent Paulos off to the coast, but Silas and Timotheos both stayed.
\v 15 The ones guiding Paulos took him to Athainai before they headed back with \add his\add* instructions for Silas and Timotheos to come and join him \add again\add* as soon as possible.
\s1 People of Athainai like new ideas
\p
\v 16 While Paulos stayed in Athainai waiting for them, he was upset in his spirit as he observed how idolatrous the people were.
\v 17 So while he stayed busy \add having discussions\add* with the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* and other worshippers from the \add meeting hall\add*, he also spent time every day with anyone who just happened to be around in the marketplace.
\v 18 Some of the Epikoureios and Stoikos philosophers enjoyed discussing things with him, but others just mocked him, “What does this babbler want to talk about \add now\add*?”
\p But others said, “Seems that he’s talking about strange demons,” because he was preaching the good message about \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* and \add coming back to life\add*.
\v 19 \add So\add* they grabbed him and brought him to the Areios Hill \add where the council sat\add* and asked, “Are we allowed to know what this new teaching that you’re bringing is about?
\v 20 It seems that you have some surprising ideas for our ears, so we want to hear what it’s all about.”
\v 21 (Everyone in Athainai and even visitors often took the opportunity either to explain or to listen to any new things.)
\p
\v 22 \add So\add* Paulos stood up in the middle of the gathering on the hill and spoke, “Men of Athainai, I’ve noticed how you all are very religious,
\v 23 because as I was looking around and observing your objects of worship, I even discovered an altar that was inscribed, ‘To the unknown \nd god\nd*.’ So without knowing it, you already worship this \nd God\nd* that I’m going to tell you about.
\v 24 This \nd God\nd* who created the earth and everything in it is \nd Yahweh\nd* who’s over heaven and earth. He doesn’t live in man-made temples
\v 25 nor can he be repaired by any human hands and he doesn’t need to be fed. \add On the contrary\add*, he’s the one who gives life and breath and who gives us everything that we need.
\v 26 From \add one man\add* he made every nationality that lives across all the earth and designated their boundaries and their futures.
\v 27 He made them so that they would try to discover \nd God\nd*, and if they did search for him they might find him as he’s not far from any one of us
\v 28 because
\q1 ‘We live in him and are moved by him and exist through him.’
\m Even some of your own poets wrote,
\q1 ‘We are all descended from just one.’
\m
\v 29 So since we are \nd God’s\nd* offspring, we shouldn’t be putting gold or silver or stone craftsmanship in the same class as the divine.
\v 30 In fact \nd God\nd* overlooked times of such ignorance in the past, but now he’s informing everyone and requiring them to repent
\v 31 because he’s set a date for when he’s going to be fairly judging the world. He’s already appointed the judge who everyone can have faith in because he \add brought him back to life\add* from the dead.
\p
\v 32 When they heard about a dead \add person\add* \add coming back to life\add*, some started mocking but others said, “We’d like to hear more about this from you another time.”
\v 33 So Paulos left at that stage
\v 34 but some people joined with him and believed, including Dionusios who was a council member, a woman named Damaris, and others as well.
\c 18
\s1 Paulos resides in Korinthos for some time
\p
\v 1 After this, \add Paulos\add* left Athainai and travelled to Korinthos.
\v 2 There he came across a Yehudi \add (Jew)\add* named Akulas \add (Aquila)\add* who had been born in Pontos but who had recently moved from Italia along with his wife Priskilla because \add Emperor\add* Klaudios had ordered all Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* to leave Romai. So \add Paulos\add* approached them,
\v 3 and since they worked in the same trade, he ended up staying with them and they worked together in \add their\add* craft of tent-making.
\v 4 On every Rest Day, \add Paulos\add* debated in the \add Jewish meeting hall\add*, and was persuading both Yehudim and Hellains \add (Greeks)\add*.
\p
\v 5 However when Silas and Timotheos arrived from Makedonia, Paulos was able to devote his full time to the message—demonstrating to the Yehudim that \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* is the \add promised\add* \nd messiah\nd*.
\v 6 But when they opposed \add the message\add* and made false accusations, he \add publicly\add* brushed \add the dust\add* off his clothes\f + \fr 18:6 \ft In the culture, this was a demonstration that you’re not responsible for their decision(s).\f* and said, “Your future deaths are your own problem. I’m innocent \add of your guilt.\add* From now on, I’ll going to the non-Jews.”
\v 7 \add So\add* \add Paulos\add* left there, and went to stay at the house of a man named Titos Yustos who worshipped \nd God\nd* and whose house was next-door to the \add meeting hall\add*.
\v 8 Krispos the \add meeting hall\add* leader and all his household \add decided to follow\add* the \nd master\nd*, and many of the Korinthios believed after they heard \add the message\add* and were immersed.
\p
\v 9 Then the \nd master\nd* spoke to Paulos one night in a vision, “Don’t be afraid and stay silent, but keep speaking out
\v 10 because I am with you and no one can mistreat you here because there are many of my people in this city.”
\v 11 \add So\add* \add Paulos\add* stayed on for eighteen months, teaching \nd God’s\nd* message to the people there.
\p
\v 12 Then at the time when Gallion was the proconsul of Akaia, the Yehudim united against Paulos and brought a case to the tribunal against him
\v 13 saying, “This man is going against the law by persuading people to be worshipping \add his\add* \nd God\nd*.”
\p
\v 14 But as Paulos opened his mouth to speak, Gallion said to the Yehudim, “I would have accepted this charge if it was a serious wrong or evil crime and would have listened to you Yehudim.
\v 15 However, since it’s a \add question\add* about a message and names and your own law, you all can see to it yourselves because I don’t want to get involved in judging things like that.”
\v 16 \add So\add* he sent them away from the tribunal,
\v 17 but they grabbed Sosthenes, the leader from the \add meeting hall\add*, and beat him up right there \add in front of\add* the tribunal. But Gallion took action to stop them.
\s1 Paulos returns to Arami Antiokeia
\p
\v 18 Paulos stayed on for quite a few days after that, then he farewelled the believers and went to Kegkreai where he had his hair cut as was the custom for someone making a vow. Then taking Priskilla and Akulas \add (Aquila)\add* with him, he sailed towards Aram \add (Syria)\add*.
\v 19 When they arrived at Efesos, he left the other two there while he went into the \add Jewish meeting hall\add* and \add had a discussion\add* with the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* there.
\v 20 They asked him to remain longer, but he turned them down
\v 21 although as he gave his farewells, he said, “I’ll come back here again if \nd God\nd* wills it,” then he sailed on from Efesos.
\p
\v 22 When he landed at Kaisareia, he went \add to Yerushalem\add* \add (Jerusalem)\add* and greeted the assembly there before continuing on to Antiokeia.
\v 23 He stayed there for some time before travelling through the \add regions\add* of Galatia and Frugia strengthening \add the faith of\add* the believers \add through his teaching\add*.
\s1 Apollos teaches in Efesos and Korinthos
\p
\v 24 Meanwhile a Jewish man named Apollos (who had been born in \add the city of\add* Alexandria) arrived in Efesos. He knew the \add Hebrew\add* scriptures well
\v 25 and had been taught about the \nd master’s\nd* way. Being a very enthusiastic person, he started teaching about \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, even though he didn’t know about being filled with the spirit.\x + \xo 18:25: \xt 2:1-4.\x*
\v 26 \add So\add* \add Apollos\add* started speaking out boldly in the \add Jewish meeting hall\add*. Priskilla and Akulas \add (Aquila)\add* listened to him, then asked to speak with him privately and explained \nd God’s\nd* way more accurately.
\v 27 Now \add Apollos\add* wanted to go to Akaia, so the believers encouraged him and sent a letter ahead of him to tell the believers there to welcome him, so when he arrived there, he was able to contribute a lot to those who through \add God’s\add* grace had also become believers.
\v 28 \add Apollos\add* was \add also\add* able to thoroughly refute the arguments of the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* and used the scriptures to show that \nd Yeshua\nd* is the \nd messiah\nd*.
\c 19
\s1 Many filled with the spirit in Efesos
\p
\v 1 \add So\add* then while Apollos was at Korinthos, Paulos travelled through the interior regions before arriving at Efesos. He found some followers there,
\v 2 and asked them if \add they\add* had received \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* when \add they\add* had \add first\add* believed \add in Yeshua\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*.
\p They replied, “We hadn’t even heard that there’s a holy \nd spirit\nd*.”
\p
\v 3 “So then what were you immersed into?” asked \add Paulos\add*.
\p “With Yohan’s \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add* immersion,” they answered.
\p
\v 4 Then Paulos said, “Yohan immersed people \add in water\add* with an immersion that went with repentance \add from their sins\add*, but he told them to believe in the one who would follow him, that is \add to believe\add* in \nd Yeshua\nd*.”
\p
\v 5 When the followers heard this, they were immersed \add in water again, this time proclaiming their faith\add* in \nd Yeshua\nd* the \nd master\nd*.
\v 6 Then Paulos placed his hands on them \add and prayed\add* and the holy \nd spirit\nd* came \add down\add* onto them and they started speaking in other languages and speaking messages from God.
\v 7 There were about twelve men in the group.
\p
\v 8 \add So\add* \add Paulos\add* started going to the \add Jewish meeting hall\add* and for the next three months he boldly discussed and persuaded them about \nd God’s\nd* kingdom.
\v 9 But as some of them refused to believe and became hardened against his teaching and started telling everyone that the \add Yeshua\add* way was evil, \add Paulos\add* stopped teaching in the meeting hall and taught the followers at Turannos’ school instead.
\v 10 This went on for two years to the point that everyone in Asia \add Minor\add*, whether Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* or Hellains \add (Greeks)\add*, heard the message about Yeshua.
\s1 The power of the master’s message
\p
\v 11 And \nd God\nd* was doing incredible miracles through Paulos
\v 12 such that even if handkerchiefs or aprons that had touched his skin were placed on top of any person who was sick or troubled with demons, then they would recover and the evil spirits would leave them.
\v 13 But some of the Jewish exorcists going around attempted to use the name of \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* to drive out evil spirits by telling them, “I am imploring you all \nd by Yeshua\nd* that Paulos preaches about.”
\v 14 Seven sons of the Jewish chief priest, Skeuas were doing this.
\p
\v 15 But the evil spirit would answer them saying, “I know \nd Yeshua\nd* and I recognise Paulos, but who are you?”
\p
\v 16 Then the man who had the evil spirit would jump on them and beat them up so they’d end up escaping out of the house naked and wounded.
\v 17 When everyone in Efesos heard about this, both Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* and Hellains \add (Greeks)\add* were frightened, and the name of \nd Yeshua\nd* the \nd master\nd* was being honoured.
\v 18 Many of the people who believed \add in Yeshua\add* came and confessed \add their sins\add* and wrong practices.
\v 19 Lots of the people who’d practiced magic brought their scrolls and burnt them \add in front of\add* the others. When the values of all of these were added up, it was \add huge\add*—worth around 50,000 silver \add coins\add*.
\v 20 So that was how the \nd master’s\nd* message had a powerful effect and won over many people.
\s1 The silversmiths start a ruckus in Efesos
\p
\v 21 After all of this, Paulos decided in \add his\add* \nd spirit\nd* that he would need to travel through Makedonia and Akaia enroute to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, saying, “After I’ve been there a while, I will also need to go to Romai.”
\v 22 He sent two of his workers, Timotheos and Erastos, to Makedonia while he stayed on a bit longer in Asia \add Minor\add*.
\p
\v 23 But while he was still there, a major disturbance hit the Yeshua \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* followers.
\v 24 A silversmith named Demetrios made \add little\add* silver shrines of Artemis and this business brought a tidy profit to the \add local\add* craftsmen.
\v 25 \add Demetrios\add* gathered all the workers together and told them, “Men, you all know that our prosperity comes from this business of ours.
\v 26 But you’ve seen yourselves and heard from others that not only Efesos, but almost our entire \add province of\add* Asia \add Minor\add* has become persuaded by this Paulos fellow, and \add now\add* the \add crowds\add* are saying that what we make are not \add real\add* gods.
\v 27 However it’s not only us and our trade that is coming into disrepute, but the temple of the great goddess Artemis is considered to be useless. This could result in a loss of power for the great \add Artemis\add* that all of Asia \add Minor\add* and the inhabited world have been worshipping.”
\p
\v 28 When the gathering heard this, they \add became very angry\add* and yelled out, “Artemis of the people from Efesos is great!”
\v 29 Then the \add whole\add* city was filled with confusion and the people rushed to the theatre and grabbed Gaios and Aristarkos from Makedonia—travelling companions of Paulos’s.
\v 30 Now Paulos wanted to go there \add and speak\add* to the crowd, but the believers wouldn’t let him go.
\v 31 Also some of the leaders from Asia \add Minor\add* who were friendly towards him, sent messengers to him to implore him not to go to the theatre.
\v 32 Some of the people gathered there were yelling out something, but overall it was just confusion, and the majority of the people couldn’t even tell you the reason why they were there.
\v 33 Some people in the crowd selected Alexandros \add (Alexander)\add* the Yehudi \add (Jew)\add* and sent him forward to speak, so \add he\add* motioned with his hand ready to explain the situation to the people.
\v 34 But as soon as they recognised that he was a Yehudi, people started yelling, “Artemis of the people from Efesos is great,” and this continued for over two hours.
\p
\v 35 \add Eventually\add* the city administrator was able to calm the crowd and tell them, “Men, fellow people from Efesos, what person from around here doesn’t know that it’s Efesos that’s the temple keeper of the great Artemis and of this image that fell down from Zeus?
\v 36 Since all of this is unquestionable, it makes sense for everyone to calm down and not do anything reckless.
\v 37 These men that you presented here haven’t stolen anything from the temple, nor have they slandered our goddess.
\v 38 So if Demetrios and the silver craftsmen with him have a case against anyone, they should bring it to the courts and the proconsuls and register the charges.
\v 39 But for the rest of you, if you have a grievance against others, it needs to be settled in a lawful assembly
\v 40 because we’re in danger of being charged with insurrection about what happened today. And we would have no defence that we could respond with concerning this disorderly gathering.”
\v 41 Then he told them all to go home.
\c 20
\s1 Paulos travels to Makedonia and Akaia
\p
\v 1 After all the commotion had \add died down\add*, Paulos sent for the believers, and after greeting and encouraging them, he left \add Efesos\add* to go to Makedonia.\f + \fr 20:1 \ft As he’d already decided—see 19:21.\f*
\v 2 As he travelled through the districts, he challenged the people there with a lot of messages, until he \add eventually\add* arrived in Yavan \add (Greece)\add*.
\v 3 He’d been there about three months when he heard about a plot by some Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* to waylay him as he boarded a ship to return to Aram \add (Syria)\add*, so instead he \add decided\add* to return through Makedonia
\v 4 with Sopatros son of Puros from Beroia, Aristarkos and Sekundos from Thessalonikai, Gaios from Derbai, Tukikos and Trofimos from the \add province of\add* Asia \add Minor\add*, and Timotheos all accompanying him.
\v 5 They went ahead and waited for us\f + \fr 20:5 \ft Luke (the writer) includes himself here.\f* at Troias.
\v 6 \add So\add* we sailed out from Filippou \add (Philippi)\add* after the \it Flat Bread Celebration\it*. We arrived and meet the others there at Troias after a five-day trip, and then stayed on there for seven days.
\s1 Paulos’s all-night discussions in Troias
\p
\v 7 On the Sunday we had gathered together to break bread together and Paulos was discussing many things with them. As he was scheduled to leave the next day, the meeting went on towards midnight
\v 8 and there were many lamps in the upper room where we had all gathered.
\v 9 Now there was a young man named Eutukos who was sitting on the window sill, and as Paulos went on, he fell asleep and toppled down from the third storey. The ones who reached him first thought he was dead,
\v 10 but Paulos had come downstairs and bent over \add Eutukos\add* and held him in his arms and said to the crowd, “Don’t worry, he’s still alive.”
\v 11 Then \add Paulos\add* went back up and broke the bread with the others, eating a little and continuing to talk with them until leaving at daybreak.
\v 12 The locals took the young man home and were very relieved that he was alive.
\s1 Sailing from Troias to Milaitos
\p
\v 13 We went ahead to the ship and departed for Assos where it had been arranged that we’d pick up Paulos because he wanted to walk there.
\v 14 \add So\add* when we met him at Assos, he boarded the ship and we all sailed on to Mitulainai.
\v 15 After we left \add Mitulainai\add*, we were out from Kios the next day, in Samos the day after that, and then the following day we arrived at Milaitos.
\v 16 Paulos had decided to sail past Efesos because he was hurrying to try to get to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* for the Day of Pentecost so he didn’t want to spend time in Asia \add Minor\add*.
\s1 Paulos’s farewell to the Efesian elders
\p
\v 17 \add So\add* while we were at Milaitos, he sent a message to Efesos for the elders of the assembly to come to him.
\v 18 When they arrived, he told them, “You all know that from the very first day that I set foot in Asia \add Minor\add* and for the entire time that I was there
\v 19 I humbly served the \nd master\nd* even through tears and trials when the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* plotted to destroy me.
\v 20 But I didn’t shrink back from teaching you everything that would help you, whether I was teaching in public or in individual homes.
\v 21 I explained to both Yehudim and Hellains \add (Greeks)\add* how they needed to \add turn their backs on their sins\add* and towards \nd God\nd* with faith in our \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*.
\p
\v 22 However the \nd spirit\nd* is now forcing me towards Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* even though I don’t know what will happen to me there.
\v 23 I just know that \add recently\add* in every city, the holy \nd spirit\nd* tells me that chains and hardships are waiting for me.
\v 24 But I’ve never regarded my own life as being valuable—I only want to reach the end of my life having served in the way that the \nd master Yeshua\nd* instructed me to speak out the good message about \nd God’s\nd* grace.
\p
\v 25 \add So\add* you see, I know that all of you from here where I passed through proclaiming \add God’s\add* kingdom, you will never see me again.
\v 26 That’s why I’m declaring to all of you today that I’m not guilty of your judgement
\v 27 because I didn’t avoid telling you anything that I had from \nd God\nd*.
\v 28 So \add now\add* you \add elders\add* \add look after\add* your own lives and those of \add your\add* flock because the holy \nd spirit\nd* chose you as overseers to shepherd the assembly of \nd God’s\nd* people who he paid for with his very own blood.
\v 29 I’m aware that after I leave, vicious wolves will come in amongst you and trying to steal the flock.
\v 30 Even some of you will rise up and teach a message that’s warped but which will draw some of the believers away to follow it,
\v 31 so \add take great care\add*. Remember that for three years, I never stopped warning each of you along with \add my\add* tears
\v 32 \add so\add* now I’m leaving you all in \nd God’s\nd* hands and with the message of his grace which is quite capable of helping you grow to receive the inheritance that’s for all those who’ve been purified.
\p
\v 33 I never envied anyone’s gold or silver or \add fancy\add* clothes
\v 34 and you yourselves know that I laboured with \add my\add* own hands to cover my expenses and for those with me.
\v 35 Yes, I showed you all that it’s good to work and to take care of the weaker ones and to remember the \add message\add* of the \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* when he said that it’s better to be a giver than a taker.”
\p
\v 36 When he’d finished speaking, \add Paulos\add* knelt down and prayed with them all.
\v 37 Everyone was sobbing as they hugged and kissed him
\v 38 because what he’d said about never seeing him again had really distressed them. Then they accompanied him to \add his\add* ship.
\c 21
\s1 Paulos goes to Yerushalem
\p
\v 1 When it was time for us to sail, we left them and sailed directly across to Kos before going on to Rodos the next day and then on to Patara.
\v 2 From there we found a ship heading to Foinikai, so we boarded that and set off.
\v 3 After sighting Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add* and then leaving it behind on our left, we sailed on to Aram \add (Syria)\add* and docked there at the port of Tsor \add (Tyre)\add* where the ship had cargo to be unloaded.
\v 4 We searched for the believers there and then stayed on with them for seven days. They were inspired by the \nd spirit\nd* to tell Paulos not to go to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 5 Once our time was up there and everyone knew that we were leaving, even the wives and children accompanied us out of the city to the port. We all knelt on the beach and prayed
\v 6 before we continued on and boarded \add our\add* ship and they went back to their own homes.
\p
\v 7 \add So\add* we sailed from Tsor down to Ptolemais where we were able to greet the \add brothers and sisters\add* and stay overnight with them.
\v 8 The next day we continued on to Kaisareia and ended up in Filippos’s \add (Philip)\add* house. He was one of the \add original\add* seven \add men who had been given the responsibility of distributing aid\add*—a preacher of the good message, and we stayed with him.
\v 9 \add Filippos\add* had four unmarried daughters \add still at home\add*, and they shared messages from God.
\v 10 Several days later a man named Agabos came from Yehudah \add (Judea)\add*. He too shared messages from God
\v 11 and when he arrived, he picked up Paulos’s belt and tied it around his own feet and hands, telling them that the holy \nd spirit\nd* had told him that this is how the owner of the belt would be treated by the Jewish leaders in Yerushalem before they would then hand him over to the non-Jews to be dealt with.
\p
\v 12 When we heard this described, both we and the locals begged \add Paulos\add* not to go to Yerushalem
\v 13 but \add he\add* answered, “Oh come on, what’s all this weeping and carrying on that’s breaking my heart? Actually I’m quite ready not only to be tied up, but even to be killed in Yerushalem as a testimony to the \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*.”
\p
\v 14 When we saw that we couldn’t \add change his mind\add*, we didn’t say anything else, figuring out that whatever the \nd master\nd* wills for the situation would happen,
\v 15 and so after these several days, we had gotten everything ready and started off for Yerushalem.
\v 16 Some of the believers from Kaisareia accompanied us and led us to the home of a man named Mnason to stay—he was an older believer from Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add*.
\s1 Paulos visits Ya’akov and makes a vow
\p
\v 17 When we arrived at Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, the \add brothers and sisters\add* there happily welcomed us,
\v 18 and the next day, we accompanied Paulos to go and see Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add*\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Yacob is commonly (but wrongly) called James in most older English Bibles.\f* and the elders of the assembly there.
\v 19 After greeting them, \add Paulos\add* then described all the things that \nd God\nd* had done among the non-Jews as he had served among them,
\v 20 and when they’d heard all that they praised \nd God\nd* and told \add Paulos\add*, “Brother, you’ve noticed how many thousands of Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* have believed \add in the messiah\add* but they \add still\add* strive to observe \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* \add laws\add*.
\v 21 But they’ve heard reports that you’ve been teaching the Yehudim that live outside our country that they don’t have to obey what Mosheh \add wrote\add*—telling them that they don’t have to have their children circumcised and that they don’t have to follow \add our\add* \add Jewish\add* customs.
\v 22 What will happen \add now\add*, because \add the leaders here\add* will certainly hear that you have arrived?
\v 23 So here’s what we suggest that you do: Four of our men are making a \add public\add* vow.
\v 24 \add Why don’t\add* you join these four and join them in the \add purification\add* ceremony \add at the temple\add* and pay their expenses in getting your \add heads\add* shaved. Then everyone will be able to see that it’s false what they’ve heard about you, because you \add clearly\add* \add still\add* comply with \add Mosheh’s\add* instructions.
\v 25 And as for the believers who are non-Jews, we’ve informed them of our recommendation that they should avoid anything that’s been offered to an idol, avoid consuming blood and meat from strangled animals and birds, and avoid sexual immorality.”
\p
\v 26 So the next day Paulos took the \add four\add* men for the purification ceremony in the temple and there they announced the date when their vows \add would be fulfilled\add* and their sacrifices made.
\s1 Paulos is captured inside the temple
\p
\v 27 When the seven days \add of the vow\add* were almost over, some Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* from \add the province of\add* Asia \add Minor\add* noticed \add Paulos\add* in the temple. They stirred up the crowd and grabbed hold of him,
\v 28 yelling out, “Men of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*. Help us here. This is the man who’s been teaching all around the world against Yisrael and against \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* law and against this temple. What’s more he brought non-Jews here into the temple and so has defiled this holy place.”
\v 29 (\add They said this\add* because they had previously seen Trofimos from Efesos in the city with Paulos and had assumed that he had brought him into the temple.)
\p
\v 30 \add So\add* now the whole city was in an uproar, and the crowds rushed in, grabbed Paulos, and dragged him out of the temple and hurriedly closed the temple gates.
\v 31 They were ready to kill \add Paulos\add*, but the commander of the \add Romaios\add* soldiers had heard that all Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* was in an uproar.
\v 32 Taking some soldiers and officers, he immediately ran down to the temple, and when the crowd saw the commander and the soldiers they stopped beating Paulos.
\v 33 The commander came close and took hold of \add Paulos\add* and ordered that he be tied up with a pair of chains while he tried to \add find out\add* who he was and what he’d done wrong.
\v 34 Various people in the crowd were shouting out different \add things\add*, and not being able to make any sense of it because of all the commotion, the commander ordered that \add Paulos\add* be taken into the barracks.
\v 35 When they got to the stairs, the soldiers had to lift him up above their heads because of the frenzy of the crowd,
\v 36 because most of the people were following and shouting out, “Do away with him!”
\s1 Paulos’s defence
\p
\v 37 As they were able to carry him into the fortress, Paulos called down to the commander, “Can I say something to you?”
\p “You speak Greek!”, replied the commander.
\v 38 “Aren’t you the Mitsri \add (Egyptian)\add* who stirred up a group of assassins a while back and led them out into the wilderness?”
\p
\v 39 “No, I’m a Yehudi \add (Jew)\add* from Tarsos in Kilikia—a citizen of an important city,” said Paulos. “But please, let me speak to these people.”
\p
\v 40 The commander nodded, so the soldiers stood Paulos on the stairs where he gestured to the people. The crowd quietened down, and so \add Paulos\add* spoke loudly in his Hebrew language:
\c 22
\nb
\v 1 “Men, brothers, and fathers, listen to what I have to say for myself.”
\v 2 When they heard him speaking Hebrew, they became even more \add quiet\add* as he continued,
\v 3 “I am a Yehudi who grew up in this very city to study under Gamliel. I’ve been raised according to every part of our ancestral law, working hard to obey \nd God\nd* like all of you here today.
\v 4 I used to persecute the followers of ‘The Path’ even to death—tying up both men and women and handing them over to the prisons
\v 5 as the Chief Priest and all the council of elders can tell \add you\add*. I took letters from them to \add our\add* \add Jewish\add* brothers in Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* when I went so that I could arrest the ones there and bring them back here to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* to be punished.
\s1 Paulos explains about his conversion
\r (Acts 9:1-19; 26:12-18)
\p
\v 6 “But one time as I was travelling and getting close to Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* by the middle of the day, suddenly a bright light from the sky began to flash around me
\v 7 and I fell to the ground. I heard a voice asking me, ‘Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*, Sha’ul, why are you persecuting me?’
\v 8 ‘Who are you, master?’ I answered. And the voice said, ‘I am \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*—the one from Nazaret that you are persecuting.’
\v 9 The other people with me saw the light but didn’t hear the voice that spoke to me.
\v 10 Then I asked, ‘What do I need to do, \nd master\nd*?’ ‘Get up and continue into Damesek,’ the \nd master\nd* said. ‘When you get there, you’ll be told everything that you’ve been assigned to do.’
\v 11 But I couldn’t see anything because the brightness of the light \add had affected my eyes\add*, so my companions had to lead me by hand into Damesek.
\p
\v 12 “In Damesek there was a man named Hananyah \add (Ananias; Grk. Hananias)\add*—a \add non-Jew\add* but who was respected by the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* there because he strove to obey \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* instructions about living.
\v 13 \add Hananyah\add* came to where I was and standing by me said, “Brother Sha’ul, receive your sight!” That very hour I was able to see again and look at him
\v 14 and he said, ‘The \nd god\nd* of our ancestors has \add decided in advance\add* to reveal his plans to you, and for you to see the sinless one—to hear him speaking
\v 15 because he wants you to inform people everywhere what you’ve seen and heard.
\v 16 \add So\add* what else are you waiting for? Get up and wash away your sins by getting immersed \add in water\add* as you request his forgiveness.’
\s1 The crowd riots when Paulos mentions preaching to non-Jews
\p
\v 17 “Then when I returned to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* and was praying in the temple, I fell into a trance
\v 18 and saw \add the master\add* telling me, ‘Get out of Yerushalem in a hurry because they won’t accept what you’re teaching them about me.’
\v 19 ‘\nd Master\nd*,’ I replied, ‘they know that previously I was the one imprisoning and beating the people who believed in you,
\v 20 and when Stefanos was killed after teaching about you, it was me standing there and cheering on the ones executing him as I held their coats for them.’
\v 21 Then \add the master\add* told me, ‘Get going \add now\add* because I’m sending you away to the non-Jews.’ ”
\p
\v 22 Until this point in \add his\add* message, the crowd had been listening to \add Paulos\add*, but now they started shouting, “Do away with this man! He doesn’t deserve to live in this world!”
\v 23 Then they yelled and threw some of their clothes down onto the ground and threw dust up into the air.
\v 24 So the commander ordered \add Paulos\add* to be brought inside into barracks and ordered that he be flogged and interrogated to determine what he’d done that would make the crowd react like that.
\v 25 But as they strapped him down and stretched him out for the flogging, Paulos asked the centurion standing there, “It is legal for you to flog a Romaios citizen who hasn’t even been tried?”
\p
\v 26 When he heard that, the centurion went to the commander and reported, “How are \add we\add* going to handle this, because this man is a Romaios citizen?”
\p
\v 27 \add So\add* the commander went in to where \add Paulos\add* was and asked him, “Tell me the truth. Are you a Romaios citizen?”
\p “Yes I am,” said \add Paulos\add*.
\p
\v 28 “Yeah, and I paid a lot for mine as well,” mocked the commander.
\p “Actually mine is through birth,” Paulos responded.
\p
\v 29 At this point, the soldiers who were about to interrogate \add Paulos\add* left the room, and even the commander was now afraid after learning that he’d had a Romaios citizen tied down to be flogged.
\s1 Paulos faces the Jewish council
\p
\v 30 The next day, the \add Romaios\add* commander \add still\add* wanted to know what was behind the anger of the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*, so he had \add Paulos\add* released \add from the cell\add*, but brought him down \add to a room\add* where he’d ordered the chief priests and the \add Jewish\add* council to convene. Paulos was given a seat
\c 23
\nb
\v 1 and looking directly at the council members said, “Men, brothers, all my life I have lived before \nd God\nd* as a good citizen and with a clean conscience.”
\v 2 But Hananyah \add (Ananias; Grk. Hananias)\add* the chief priest commanded one of his companions to whack \add Paulos\add* on the mouth.
\v 3 “\nd God\nd* will strike you,” said Paulos, “you grand show-off. You’ve been brought here to judge me as someone who knows the law and yet you violate the law by commanding that I be hit in the face.”
\p
\v 4 But the others standing there said, “Are you insulting \nd God’s\nd* chief priest?”
\p
\v 5 “Brothers,” said Paulos, “I didn’t realise that he’s the chief priest, because the scriptures teach us not to speak wrongly of \add our\add* leaders.”\x + \xo 23:5: \xt Exo 22:28.\x*
\p
\v 6 But then, knowing that the council consisted of some from the sect of the Saddoukaios \add (Sadducees)\add* and some from the Farisaios’ party, Paulos continued loudly, “Men, brothers, like my father, I’m a member of the Farisaios and I’m being tried today concerning the hope and the resurrection of the dead!”
\p
\v 7 Well, as soon as he said this, the council was split as the Farisaios and the Saddoukaios started arguing.
\v 8 (Saddoukaios say that the dead don’t \add come back to life\add*, and there’s no \add messengers\add* and no \add spirits\add*, but the Farisaios believe there is.)
\v 9 \add So\add* then there was a big kerfuffle because some of the teachers of the law spoke up for the Farisaios saying, “We don’t see anything wrong with this man. Maybe it was a spirit or one \add of\add* \add God’s\add* \add messengers\add* that spoke to him.”
\p
\v 10 But the \add Romaios\add* commander was afraid that they might tear Paulos apart in the big argument, so he called for his soldiers to come down to uplift \add Paulos\add* and return him to the barracks.
\p
\v 11 The following night, the \nd master\nd* appeared to \add Paulos\add* and told him, “Be brave because just like you testified about me here in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, so too you’ll tell them about me in Romai.”
\s1 The plan to kill Paulos
\p
\v 12 The next day, some of the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* gathered informally and pledged not to eat or drink until they’d killed Paulos.
\v 13 There were more than forty of them in this conspiracy,
\v 14 and they approached the chief priests and elders and told them, “We’ve \add made an oath\add* to taste nothing until we’ve killed Paulos, otherwise we’ll bring a curse on ourselves.
\v 15 So you guys assemble the council and report to the commander so that he’ll bring him down to you all as if you need more information about his actions, and then we’ll have an opportunity to kill before he gets here.
\p
\v 16 But a nephew of Paulos had heard about this ambush and went in to the barracks to inform Paulos.
\v 17 Paulos then called one of the Romaios centurions and told him to take the young man to the commander because he had something to tell him.
\v 18 So the centurion took him to the commander, explaining, “The prisoner Paulos, called me and asked me to bring this young man to you because he has something to report to you.”
\p
\v 19 The commander took the young man by the hand and led him into his private office and asked, “What do you have to tell me?”
\p
\v 20 “The Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* have decided to ask you,” he said, “that tomorrow you bring Paulos down to the council as if they wanted to question him further.
\v 21 But don’t listen to them because over forty of their men will be lying in wait for him, and they’ve sworn not to eat or drink until they’ve killed him. In fact, they’re all set up already, just waiting for your consent.”
\p
\v 22 The commander told the young man not to tell anyone else about the ambush and dismissed him.
\s1 Paulos is escorted away in the night
\p
\v 23 Then the commander called in two of \add his\add* centurions and ordered them to prepare two-hundred soldiers to travel as far as Kaisareia, supported by seventy mounted soldiers and two-hundred more with spears. They were to be ready to leave by 9pm,
\v 24 along with a horse for Paulos to ride, and were to get him safely to Governor Felix.
\v 25 Then the commander wrote a letter ready to take:
\p
\v 26 “Greetings to the most excellent Governor Felix from Klaudios Lusias.
\v 27 I’m writing to you about this man who had been captured by the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* here and they were about to kill him when I moved in with my soldiers because he is a Romaios citizen.
\v 28 I wanted to know what they were accusing him of, so I took him down to their council meeting,
\v 29 but I discovered that it was just some detail about their \add Jewish\add* law and nothing that should result in death or imprisonment.
\v 30 Then when I learnt that they have a plot to try to ambush and kill him, I sent him immediately to you, and I’ll order his accusers to bring their case before you.”
\p
\v 31 So the soldiers carried out their instructions and took Paulos as far as Antipatris during the night.
\v 32 The next morning the foot soldiers returned to the barracks, leaving the mounted soldiers to take him on
\v 33 to Kaisareia, where they presented Paulos to the governor along with the letter.
\v 34 After reading the letter the governor asked \add Paulos\add* what province that he’s from and \add found out\add* that he was from Kilikia.
\v 35 Then he ordered \add Paulos\add* to be kept under guard \add in the prison\add* in Herodes’s \add (Herod)\add* palace until he would hear from him in the future once his accusers got there.
\c 24
\s1 The Yehudim accuse Paulos before Governor Felix
\p
\v 1 Five days later, Hananyah \add (Ananias; Grk. Hananias)\add* the chief priest arrived along with some elders and an attorney named Tertullos, to bring the charges against Paulos before Governor \add Felix\add*.
\v 2 Once he was called to speak, Tertullos began his \add accusation\add* of \add Paulos\add*:
\p “We have seen peace under your rule and the helpful reforms that this nation has experienced through your provision,
\v 3 most excellent Felix, and we gratefully welcome everything you’ve done and everywhere you’ve done it.
\v 4 But in order not to oblige you longer than necessary, in your gentleness we request you to listen to our brief statement.
\v 5 This man here has been found to be a troublemaker, stirring up riots among the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* throughout the inhabited world, and as a ring-leader of the Nazoraios sect
\v 6 he even attempted to profane the temple where we were able to arrest him.
\v 7 ◘
\v 8 When you examine him yourself you’ll discover all these \add things\add* that we’re accusing him of.”
\v 9 The Yehudim who had come also affirmed all the accusations.
\s1 Paulos’s defence against the accusations
\p
\v 10 \add So\add* the governor nodded for Paulos to speak and he started,
\p “I happily defend myself today knowing that you have many years of experience ruling \add in this region\add*.
\v 11 You can \add quickly\add* establish that it was less than twelve days ago that I arrived in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* to worship there.
\v 12 They never found me in the temple arguing with anyone or stirring up the crowd, and nor was I doing that in the \add Jewish meeting halls\add* or anywhere in the city
\v 13 and so they can’t provide evidence to you to support any of their \add accusations\add*.
\v 14 However, I must confess that I follow ‘The Path’ that they call a sect which serves the \nd God\nd* of our ancestors and believes everything in \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* law as well as the writings of the prophets.
\v 15 We put our trust in the same \nd God\nd* that they themselves follow, and we all anticipate a resurrection of those who serve God and those who don’t.
\v 16 Because of this, I also strive to maintain a clean conscience in everything, before \nd God\nd* and the people.
\p
\v 17 After several years away, I arrived \add in Yerushalem\add* bringing offerings to help my own people and before God.
\v 18 That was when they found me in the temple after going through the \add purification\add* ceremony, and without either a crowd or a commotion around me.
\v 19 But there were some Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* from \add the province of\add* Asia \add Minor\add* who are the ones who should be appearing here before you, and they should be the ones accusing me if they could find anything against me.
\v 20 Even these ones here \add now\add* should explain what \add their\add* council found me guilty of,
\v 21 other than my calling out that I was being judged because I believe in the resurrection of the dead.
\p
\v 22 But Felix who was already familiar with followers of ‘The Path’, announced that the trial would be adjourned until Commander Lusias arrived when he would investigate further.
\v 23 He ordered the centurion to keep \add Paulos\add* confined, but under \add relaxed\add* conditions and \add allowing any\add* of his friends to bring him anything he needed.
\s1 Paulos speaks to the governor and his wife
\p
\v 24 However, several days later Felix returned there, \add this time\add* with his wife Drusilla who was Jewish. He sent for Paulos and listened to him explain about faith in \nd the messiah\nd*.
\v 25 But when the subject turned to righteousness and self-control and the coming judgement, Felix became very uncomfortable and told him, “That’s enough for now. If I have time in the future, I’ll send for you.”
\v 26 However he was also hoping that Paulos would give him money, so he often sent for him and spoke with him.
\p
\v 27 This went on for two years, until Felix was replaced \add as governor\add* by Porkios Festos, but wanting to stay in the good books of the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*, Felix left Paulos confined.
\c 25
\s1 Tried yet again, Paulos appeals to Kaisar
\p
\v 1 After Festos had been in the area for three days, he made the uphill trip from Kaisareia to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 2 There the chief priests and the Jewish leaders repeated their case against Paulos, and they implored \add Festos\add*,
\v 3 asking as a \add special\add* favour, that he would order \add Paulos\add* to be sent to Yerushalem (because they wanted to ambush and kill him on the way).
\v 4 But Festos replied that he’d keep Paulos in Kaisareia and he was heading back there soon,
\v 5 so he told them, “Some of your leaders can go there with me, and if the man has done anything wrong, they can explain their \add accusations\add* there.”
\p
\v 6 After staying seven or eight days in Yerushalem, \add Festos\add* and company travelled back to Kaisareia, and the next day he sat at the judge’s bench and ordered that Paulos be brought in.
\v 7 When he arrived, the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* who’d come from Yerushalem stood facing \add Paulos\add* and presented many heavy charges which they weren’t able to prove.
\v 8 Paulos defended himself, stating that \add he\add* hadn’t sinned against any Jewish law or against the temple, nor against any Romaios law.
\p
\v 9 However, Festos wanted to be able to grant a favour to the Yehudim, so in response to Paulos he asked, “Are you willing to be returned to Yerushalem so that I can judge you about these \add things\add* there?”
\p
\v 10 “I’m here facing Kaisar’s representative,” Paulos replied. “and it’s quite appropriate for me to be judged here. I’ve done nothing wrong to these Yehudim as you can no doubt see for yourself.
\v 11 If I had done anything wrong, anything worthy of the death sentence, then I’m \add ready to accept\add* death. On the other hand, if none of their \add accusations\add* are true, no one can hand me over to them. I appeal to Kaisar.”
\p
\v 12 So Festos conferred with his legal counsel and answered, “Well, you’ve appealed to Kaisar so you’ll go to Kaisar!”
\s1 King Agrippas wants to hear from Paulos
\p
\v 13 Several days later, King Agrippas and \add his sister\add* Bernike arrived in Kaisareia to meet Festos.
\v 14 As they \add had planned to\add* stay on for a few days, Festos mentioned about Paulos to the king, telling him, “There’s a prisoner here that Felix left behind.
\v 15 When I was in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, the chief priests and Jewish elders reported to me, wanting a conviction of this man.
\v 16 I told them that it’s not the Romaios way to convict someone until they’ve been able to face \add their\add* accusers and make \add their\add* defence against the accusations.
\v 17 So as soon as they arrived here, on the very next day I sat on the judge’s bench and ordered the man to be brought in
\v 18 that they were accusing, but they didn’t raise even one of the \add charges\add* that I expected.
\v 19 Instead they raised some issues about their own beliefs and about some dead person called \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* that Paulos reckoned was still living.
\v 20 I was puzzled about this debate and asked him if he wanted to be judged about these \add things\add* in Yerushalem.
\v 21 But Paulos appealed to be kept safe from them until he could face the emperor, so I ordered him to be kept \add in prison\add* until I can send him to Romai.”
\p
\v 22 “I’d quite like to hear him myself,” Agrippas said.
\p “Well, tomorrow,” replied Festos, “you’ll be able to hear from him.”
\p
\v 23 So the next day, \add King\add* Agrippas and Bernike formally arrived with all their attendants and entered the auditorium to greet the commanders and the \add prominent\add* men of the city, and then Festos ordered for Paulos to be brought in.
\v 24 Festos started, “King Agrippas and everyone present, observe the prisoner who a multitude of Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* pleaded with me about both in Yerushalem and here, that he doesn’t deserve to still be alive.
\v 25 But I haven’t discovered anything that he’s done that’s worthy of a death sentence, and since he himself appealed to the emperor, I judged that that’s where he should be sent.
\v 26 The problem is that I don’t have any charge to write to my master and so I’ve brought the prisoner out \add in front of\add* you so that after you \add examine\add* him, I might have something I can write,
\v 27 because it doesn’t seem logical to me to transport a prisoner \add to Romai\add* if there’s no charges specified against him.”
\c 26
\s1 Paulos’s defence before Festos and Agrippas
\p
\v 1 \add So\add* then Agrippas told Paulos, “Okay, you can give your side \add now then\add*.”
\m So Paulos gestured with his hand and began:
\p
\v 2 “Concerning all the charges which have been brought against me by the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*, King Agrippas, I consider myself fortunate
\v 3 because you’re an expert on Jewish customs and issues, so I ask you to listen to me patiently.
\p
\v 4 “All the Yehudim know about my early life, from my upbringing in my own country and then in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 5 They \add also\add* know, and could even testify to you if they would admit it, that I followed our religious laws very closely as a member of the Farisaios party.
\v 6 Yet now I stand judged for believing in the same hope as our ancestors—that hope promised by \add our\add* \nd God\nd*
\v 7 who we twelve tribes diligently serve day and night in order to obtain—the hope, \add your majesty\add*, for which I’m being indicted.
\v 8 So why do you all find it unbelievable that \nd God\nd* can raise the dead?
\p
\v 9 “I too \add originally\add* thought it necessary to do many things to oppose the cause \nd of\nd* this \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* from Nazaret.
\v 10 So in Yerushalem I worked to have many of these innocent believers locked up in \add prison\add* with the authority of the chief priests, or if they were to be killed, I would add my vote against them.
\v 11 And in all the \add Jewish meeting halls\add*, \add when I found believers\add* I punished them and forced them to curse \add God\add* and \add treated them with extreme anger\add*, even travelling to further away towns.
\s1 Paulos tells about his commission
\p
\v 12 “So it was one time travelling to Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* carrying permission letters from the chief priests
\v 13 that in the middle of the day, \add your majesty\add*, I saw a bright light from the sky—even brighter than the sun—and it shone on me and my companions.
\v 14 All of us fell down to the ground and I heard a voice speaking to me in Hebrew saying, ‘Sha’ul \add (Saul; Grk. Saulos/Saul)\add*, Sha’ul, why are you persecuting me? Life will be difficult for you if you resist me.’
\p
\v 15 ‘Who are you, master?’, I asked.
\p “And the \nd master\nd* responded, ‘I am \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, the one you’re persecuting.
\v 16 Now get up, because I let you see me in order to appoint you as \add my\add* servant and my witness—to tell the people what you’ve seen and what you will be shown.
\v 17 I will rescue you from \add your\add* own people and from the non-Jews that I’m sending you out to,
\v 18 to open their minds and for them to turn back from darkness to light and from Satan’s power to \nd God\nd*, so that they \add can be forgiven\add* for \add their\add* sins and receive an inheritance among the ones who’ve been made guiltless by \add their\add* faith in me.’
\s1 Paulos tells about his ministry
\p
\v 19 “So then, King Agrippas, I couldn’t disobey that vision
\v 20 so I preached first to those in Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* and then in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* and all of Yehudah \add (Judea)\add*, and then in \add the towns of\add* the non-Jews. I preached about turning from their sins and turning back to \nd God\nd*, and then actually living like people who had repented.
\v 21 It was because of these \add things\add* that the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* grabbed me in the temple and tried to do me in.
\v 22 So it is that with \nd God’s\nd* help until this very day I have stood testifying to commoners and leaders, teaching nothing other than what Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* and the prophets had already written that would happen—
\v 23 that the \nd messiah\nd* would \add come and\add* suffer and as the first of the dead to \add come back to life\add*, he is going to be proclaiming light to both Yehudim and non-Jews.”
\p
\v 24 Then speaking about \add Paulos’s\add* \add defence\add*, \add Governor\add* Festos said, “Paulos, you’re stark, raving mad! All your education has turned you into an idiot.”
\p
\v 25 But Paulos said, “I haven’t gone mad, most excellent Festos, but I’m speaking the truth in all seriousness.
\v 26 The king knows what I’m talking about and that’s why I’m speaking boldly, because none of these \add things\add* were done in a corner and I’m sure that none of it has escaped his notice.
\v 27 King Agrippas, do you believe what the prophets \add wrote\add*? Yes, I’m sure you do.”
\p
\v 28 But Agrippas answered Paulos, “Do you think you can talk me into becoming a follower of the messiah in just that short time?”
\p
\v 29 “I hope \nd to God\nd*”, said Paulos, “whether it takes a short time or a long time, that both you and the others listening today will become just like me except for these chains.”
\p
\v 30 Then the king and the governor both stood, along with Bernike and the others sitting with them
\v 31 and as they left they were saying to each other, “That man hasn’t done anything deserving of death or imprisonment.”
\p
\v 32 “Yes, he could have been \add set free\add*,” replied Agrippas, “if he hadn’t appealed to Kaisar.”
\c 27
\s1 Paulos is escorted off overseas
\p
\v 1 \add So\add* when it was considered the right season to sail to Italia, Paulos and some other prisoners were put under the guard of a centurion named Yulios (from the Augustan Regiment).
\v 2 We all boarded on a ship from Adramuttainos that was about to sail to various places along the coast of Asia \add Minor\add*. Aristarkos, a Makedonian man from Thessalonikai, was with us as we departed.
\p
\v 3 The next day we docked at Tsidon \add (Sidon)\add* where Yulios \add very\add* kindly allowed Paulos to visit some of his friends so they could provide for his needs.
\v 4 When we left \add again\add*, the ship stayed between Kupros \add (Cyprus)\add* and the mainland because of the unfavourable winds,
\v 5 then after sailing past Kilikia and Pamfulia, we docked at Mura in \add the province\add* of Lukia.
\v 6 There the centurion found a ship from Alexandria that was sailing to Italia, so he boarded us on that.
\p
\v 7 After many days of slow and difficult sailing, we came alongside Knidos but the wind wasn’t helpful to us, so we sailed across to Kraitai \add (Crete)\add* and along the cape of Salmone.
\v 8 We sailed along it with difficulty until we came to a place called ‘Fair Havens’ which was near the city of Lasaia.
\p
\v 9 A lot of time had now elapsed and the voyage was starting to become dangerous because we were already heading into winter. Paulos advised those in charge,
\v 10 telling them, “Men, I perceive that we’re going to encounter a lot of damage on this voyage, with loss not only of the cargo and the ship, but also our lives.”
\v 11 But the centurion was persuaded by the ship owner and the captain, rather than by what Paulos had said.
\v 12 They realised that this harbour wasn’t suitable to winter in, so the majority suggested leaving there with the expectation of being able to get to Kraitai to winter in the harbour at Foinix (which was open to both the southwest and the northwest).
\s1 The severe storm at sea
\p
\v 13 When a south wind started blowing gently, they thought they could make it, so they pulled in the anchor and sailed along close to Kraitai \add (Crete)\add*.
\v 14 However, a short while later the wind turned to a violent north-easter which forced us out to sea.
\v 15 The ship was driven by the wind and unable to tack to face into it, so the sailors gave up and just let it be driven.
\v 16 As we passed a small island called Klaudai, we got a brief break and were able to resecure the lifeboat
\v 17 and then bring it up on board. Then ropes were used to wrap around the ship in case it were to run aground into the shallows at Surtis and the gear was lowered,\f + \fr 27:17 \ft It’s not certain what was meant here—it might have meant lowering the sails further to catch less wind or even lowering the mast, or else letting out the sea anchor to help keep the back of the ship facing the wind.\f* allowing the wind to drive the ship along.
\v 18 We were violently tossed around by the storm, and the next day the crew started \add lightening the ship by\add* tipping \add some of the cargo\add* overboard.
\v 19 By the third day, the sailors themselves \add in desperation\add* were even throwing some of the ship’s gear overboard.
\v 20 We didn’t see either the sun or the stars for several more days—it wasn’t a minor storm by any means—and by then any remaining hope that we had \add of staying alive\add* disappeared
\v 21 and no one had eaten or drunk much at all.
\p Then Paulos stood up in the middle of them and said, “Well, you should have listened to me after all and not left Kraitai, so now we have this damage and loss.
\v 22 Nevertheless I think \add we\add* can all cheer up because not a single life will be lost—only the ship—
\v 23 because last night one of the \add messengers\add* \nd of\nd* the \nd God\nd* that I serve came and stood beside me
\v 24 and told me not to be afraid because I would indeed get to stand before Kaisar and that \nd God\nd* has granted your lives to me as well.
\v 25 So cheer up, fellows, because I’m certain that \nd God\nd* will carry out what was told to me
\v 26 even though we’ll run aground onto an island first.”
\p
\v 27 So it happened that in the middle of \add our\add* fourteenth night of being blown across the Adrias Sea, the sailors suspected that we were nearing land.
\v 28 They dropped a weighted line and measured the ocean depth at around 40 metres and then a bit later it was down to 30 metres.
\v 29 Worrying that the ship might run aground on rough \add rocks\add*, they threw out four anchors from the stern as they hoped that daylight would come \add quickly\add*.
\v 30 Then the sailors lowered the lifeboat as if planning to pull out some anchors from the bow, but really planning to desert the ship.
\v 31 However Paulos told the centurion and the soldiers that if the sailors didn’t remain with the ship, everybody couldn’t be sure to be saved,
\v 32 so the soldiers cut the ropes attaching the lifeboat and allowed it to run aground.
\p
\v 33 As they waited for the daylight, Paulos encouraged them all to eat something, saying, “Today is \add our\add* fourteenth day and you’ve had nothing to eat. As we’re waiting,
\v 34 I urge you to eat something. This will help you to have strength because none of you are going to drown.”
\v 35 Having said that, he gave thanks \nd to God\nd* \add in front of\add* everyone, and breaking some bread, started eating it.
\v 36 They all cheered up and also started eating.
\v 37 There were two hundred and seventy-six of them on the ship,
\v 38 and when they’d all eaten what they wanted, they lightened the ship more by throwing \add their\add* wheat into the sea.
\s1 The ship runs aground
\p
\v 39 Once it became light, they saw that they were close to shore in a bay but didn’t recognise the place. After some discussion, they decided to try to save the ship
\v 40 \add so\add* they cut away the anchors that were holding them in the sea and untied the rudder \add handles\add*. Then raising the foresail, they let the wind blow them into the shore.
\v 41 But they hit a bank with ocean on both sides of it and the ship ran aground. The bow of the ship was completely stuck but the stern was being broken up by the force of the ocean.
\p
\v 42 At this point the soldiers \add decided\add* that they should kill the prisoners in case any of them decided to swim away and escape,
\v 43 but the centurion wanted to keep Paulos safe so he vetoed that plan. He commanded those who could swim to jump off and swim in to shore,
\v 44 and for the others to grab \add floating\add* planks or something off the ship \add that would float\add*, and so everyone got safely onto the beach.
\c 28
\s1 On the island of Melitai
\p
\v 1 Once everyone was safe, we discovered that the island is called Melitai.
\v 2 And the people there showed incredible kindness to us as they welcomed us and lit a fire, as it started raining \add again\add* and was \add very\add* cold.
\v 3 However when Paulos was helping to pick up sticks and putting them on the fire, a viper was driven out by the heat and clamped its jaws down on his hand.
\v 4 When the islanders saw the snake hanging from his hand they decided that he must be a murderer who had survived the shipwreck but \add now\add* fate had \add finally\add* decided his time was up.
\v 5 However, \add Paulos\add* just shook the snake off into the fire and wasn’t harmed.
\v 6 The locals were waiting for his arm to swell up or for him to fall down dead, but after a while when nothing happened they changed their minds and decided that instead, he must be a god.
\p
\v 7 The area that we had beached in belonged to the leader of the island named Poplios. He welcomed us and \add looked after\add* us for three days,
\v 8 but while we were there, \add his\add* father got sick with dysentery and \add fever\add*. Paulos went in and placing his hands on him, prayed for him and healed him.
\v 9 As a result of that, anyone on the whole island who was sick came and was cured
\v 10 and so the people brought us many gifts and when we left, they supplied us with provisions.
\s1 Sailing from Melitai to Romai
\p
\v 11 After three months we were able to leave on an Alexandrian ship that had wintered at the island. It was decorated with carvings of the twin ‘gods’.
\v 12 We stayed three days when we arrived at Surakousai
\v 13 and then we sailed on to Raigion. The next day a south wind came so we sailed on to Potioloi
\v 14 where we found some believers and they encouraged us to stay for seven days. Then we \add finally\add* got to Romai.
\v 15 The believers in \add Romai\add* had heard about us, so they came to meet us at the Appios Market and the Three Taverns. Paulos was encouraged to see them and thanked \nd God\nd*.
\s1 Paulos preaches in Romai
\p
\v 16 When we got into Romai proper, Paulos was allowed to live by himself, with just a soldier guarding him.
\p
\v 17 After three days, he called together the leaders of the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* \add in Romai\add*, and when they’d all gathered he spoke to them, “Men, brothers, I have done nothing against \add my\add* people or against \add our\add* ancestral customs. I was taken prisoner in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* and handed over to the Romaios
\v 18 who, after examining me, wanted to release me because I had done nothing worthy of execution.
\v 19 But in defending myself against the Yehudim there, I felt compelled to appeal to Kaisar, although I myself had no complaint against the Yehudim.
\v 20 So that was why I called for you and wanted to address you, because I am in \add chains\add* for the sake of Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* hope—\add the messiah\add*.”
\p
\v 21 “We haven’t received any correspondence here about you,” they answered. “Nor have any of \add our\add* brothers \add from Yerushalem\add* arrived here to report any crimes of yours.
\v 22 But we would \add very much\add* like to hear from you about your thinking, because we’ve certainly heard about this new sect because everyone is speaking out against it.”
\p
\v 23 Then they set a day when they could visit him where he was staying and so that from morning to evening he could explain \nd about God’s\nd* kingdom and show them how Mosheh’s \add (Moses)\add* law and the prophets’ writings spoke about \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*.
\v 24 Afterwards some were persuaded while others didn’t find it believable.
\v 25 They argued back and forth, and as they were about to leave, Paulos shared this final message, “The holy \nd spirit\nd* spoke rightly through Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* the prophet to your ancestors, saying:
\q1
\v 26 Go to these people and tell them,
\q1 When you listen you will hear but not understand,
\q1 and when you look you will see but not perceive it.
\q1
\v 27 Because these people’s minds have become dull
\q2 and they can barely hear with their ears
\q2 and they shut their eyes,
\q1 just in case their eyes might \add actually\add* see,
\q2 and their ears might \add actually\add* hear,
\q2 and they might understand with their minds,
\q1 and turn back to me
\q2 and then I would heal them.\x + \xo 28:26-27: \xt Isa 6:9-10.\x*
\p
\v 28 So now let it be known to you all that the \add message of\add* salvation from \nd God\nd* has been sent out to non-Jews and they will listen.”
\p
\v 29 ◘
\v 30 \add Paulos\add* remained in his own rental accommodation in Romai for two whole years and welcomed everyone who went to see him,
\v 31 telling them \nd about God’s\nd* kingdom and boldly teaching about the \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* without any hindrance.
